// Stories for the matrix: STORIES["<civ id>:<activity>"] = "…". See cultures/README.md.
Object.assign(STORIES, {
  "britons:hunt": "The Welsh laws of Hywel Dda, set down around 940, gave the king's chief huntsman, the pencynydd, a seat in the hall and a share of every hide. A king's trained hound was valued at a full pound, as much as several cows. In autumn the hunters rode out with horn and leash into the oak valleys after the stag, knowing that the meat on the winter tables, and their own honour, rode with them.",
  "britons:battle": "Around the year 600 the men of Gododdin feasted for a year on the mead of their lord at Din Eidyn, then rode south to Catraeth to fight the Angles. The bard Aneirin sang that three hundred went, gold-torqued and laughing, and that hardly any came home. Y Gododdin is not a song of victory. It praises each fallen man by name, so that the debt of mead was paid and the memory kept.",
  "britons:gather": "In Pembrokeshire (Sir Benfro) and Ceredigion the last ears of corn in the field were plaited together and called y wrach, the hag. The reapers stood back and threw their sickles at it in turn; whoever cut it had to carry it to the farmhouse dry, while the women waited at the door with buckets of water. Harvest ended in laughter, a soaking, and a feast that paid the reapers' sweat.",
  "britons:ritual": "On Nos Galan Gaeaf, the eve of winter, Welsh villagers built a bonfire on the hill and each person threw in a white stone marked as their own. They circled the fire, then ran home before the tailless black sow could catch them. At dawn they searched the ashes. A stone that could not be found meant its owner would not see another winter. People went quietly, not in terror, to learn what the year held.",
  "britons:rain": "Imagined: drovers gather with their black cattle above Llandovery (Llanymddyfri), ready for the long road to the English markets. The mist sits thick on the Black Mountains (Y Mynydd Du) and nobody moves; the cattle could walk off the edge of a cwm. The men sit on the wall, share a pipe, and sing hymns in low voices. Rain is nothing to them. They wait only for the cloud to lift and show the path.",
  "britons:flood": "On the morning of 30 January 1607 a wall of water came up the Bristol Channel and the Severn (Hafren), drowning the low farms of Gwent and Glamorgan (Morgannwg) as far as the hills. Some two thousand people died on both shores. In the churches of Goldcliff and Redwick, brass plaques still mark how high the sea came. People said God had spoken; they rebuilt the sea walls all the same.",
  "britons:wind": "On the night of 25 October 1859 a hurricane drove the steam clipper Royal Charter, home from the Australian goldfields, onto the rocks at Moelfre on Anglesey (Ynys Môn). Some four hundred and fifty died. Men of the village formed a human chain into the surf and pulled survivors ashore. Charles Dickens came to see the place, and wrote of the parson who buried the drowned strangers as if they were his own.",
  "britons:fire": "On 18 September 1400 Owain Glyndŵr was proclaimed Prince of Wales (Tywysog Cymru) at Glyndyfrdwy, and within days his men set fire to the English borough of Ruthin (Rhuthun). Market stalls and timber houses burned while the castle held. For Welsh people it was the first spark of a long hope after a century of conquest; the bards hailed him, and the smoke over the Clwyd valley was read as the start of something.",
  "britons:thunder": "Imagined: a thunderstorm rolls over Eryri, and the shepherds of the high farms count the strokes as they gather the ewes off the slopes. An old woman says the mountain is angry with the copper miners again. The children are told to cover the mirrors. When the storm passes, a hymn rises from the chapel at the foot of the valley, half gratitude and half the habit of every Sunday.",
  "britons:birth": "On 28 January 1457, in Pembroke Castle (Castell Penfro), Margaret Beaufort, just thirteen and already a widow, gave birth to a son after a dangerous labour. She never bore another child. The Welsh bards soon sang of a mab darogan, a son of prophecy who would restore the old British crown. Few could have believed the frail boy, Henry Tudor (Harri Tudur), would end the Wars of the Roses at Bosworth.",
  "britons:death": "In the Welsh borders (y Gororau) a sin-eater was sometimes called to a funeral. John Aubrey wrote in the 1680s of a poor man who stood by the coffin, took a loaf, a bowl of beer and a sixpence passed over the corpse, and so took the dead person's sins on himself. The family then sat up through the gwylnos, the wake night, singing hymns until the walk to the churchyard.",

  "english:hunt": "On 2 August 1100 King William II, called Rufus, rode hunting in the New Forest, the royal forest his father had made by clearing villages. As the sun sank, an arrow loosed by Walter Tirel at a stag struck the king in the chest. Tirel fled to France. Charcoal burners carried the body to Winchester on a cart. Many English said the forest had taken its revenge, and few wept.",
  "english:battle": "On 25 October 1415, the feast of Saint Crispin, Henry V's tired, sick army faced a far larger French host at Agincourt. Rain had turned the ploughed field to mud. The longbowmen drove stakes, and as the French men-at-arms floundered toward them, the arrows fell like snow. By evening the French nobility lay dead in heaps. England sang 'Deo gratias Anglia' and remembered it for six hundred years.",
  "english:gather": "Thomas Tusser's 'Five Hundred Points of Good Husbandry' (1573) told English farmers how to bring in the harvest, then how to keep Harvest Home. The last load came in on the hock cart, decked with green boughs and a corn dolly, with reapers riding on top shouting. The farmer gave a supper of beef and plum pudding, and master and labourer drank together, once a year, as equals.",
  "english:ritual": "Before Elizabeth I was crowned, her adviser John Dee, mathematician and astrologer, was asked to cast the stars and choose a day. He chose 15 January 1559. Dee later spent years seeking angelic messages through a scryer, hoping to learn the future of England and the world. For most English people the question went simply to God, in the Book of Common Prayer, Sunday after Sunday.",
  "english:rain": "On 19 July 1588 the Spanish Armada was sighted off the Lizard. Legend says Francis Drake was playing bowls on Plymouth Hoe and finished his game. The truth behind it: the English fleet could not leave harbour against the wind and the incoming tide. There was nothing to do but wait for the ebb and the turn of the wind, and that night they warped their ships out into the Sound.",
  "english:flood": "On the night of 31 January 1953 a storm surge swept over the sea walls of eastern England. On Canvey Island in the Thames estuary, families woke to water rising through the floorboards; fifty-eight died there, and over three hundred along the coast. Neighbours rowed through streets in the dark. The country answered with sandbags and soldiers, and then built the Thames Barrier so it would not happen again.",
  "english:wind": "On 26 and 27 November 1703 the Great Storm crossed southern England. Chimneys fell, the lead was ripped off Westminster Abbey, and thousands of sailors drowned on the Goodwin Sands. Henry Winstanley, who had built the Eddystone lighthouse and wished to be in it during the greatest storm ever, was there that night; nothing of him or it was found. Daniel Defoe collected eyewitness letters and printed them as 'The Storm'.",
  "english:fire": "In the early hours of 2 September 1666 a fire began in Thomas Farriner's bakery in Pudding Lane. A dry east wind drove it through the timber city for four days. Samuel Pepys buried his wine and his Parmesan cheese in his garden and watched St Paul's cathedral burn. Over thirteen thousand houses were lost, yet very few people died, and Wren rebuilt the churches in stone.",
  "english:thunder": "On Sunday 21 October 1638, during afternoon service at Widecombe-in-the-Moor on Dartmoor, a great thunderstorm struck the church. A ball of fire burst through a window, part of the tower crashed through the roof, and four people died. Villagers said the Devil himself had come for a card-playing man in the congregation. Verses recording the day still hang in the church.",
  "english:birth": "On 7 September 1533 Anne Boleyn gave birth to a daughter at Greenwich. Henry VIII had broken with Rome to have a son; the jousts planned to celebrate were quietly cancelled. No one at the christening could have guessed that this girl, Elizabeth, would rule for forty-four years, face down the Armada, and give her name to an age. Her birth disappointed a king and delighted a nation later.",
  "english:death": "In English parishes a passing bell was tolled when someone died: three strokes for a child, six for a woman, nine for a man, the 'nine tailors', then a stroke for each year of their age. Everyone in the fields stopped to count. In 1624 John Donne wrote, 'never send to know for whom the bell tolls; it tolls for thee'. Then came the black cloth, the procession and the churchyard.",

  "house-of-lancaster:hunt": "Between 1406 and 1413 Edward, Duke of York, translated a French hunting book into English as 'The Master of Game' and dedicated it to Prince Henry of Lancaster, the future Henry V. It describes the hart, the boar and the hounds, and claims hunting keeps a man from idleness and sin. The prince loved the chase. Two years after his crowning, Edward died fighting beside him at Agincourt.",
  "house-of-lancaster:battle": "On Palm Sunday, 29 March 1461, the Lancastrian army met the Yorkists at Towton in a snowstorm. The wind blew the snow into Lancastrian faces; their arrows fell short while Yorkist arrows struck home. After hours of slaughter the line broke and men drowned in the Cock Beck. It may have been the bloodiest day ever fought on English soil, and it cost Henry VI his crown.",
  "house-of-lancaster:gather": "Imagined: at Michaelmas the reeves of the Duchy of Lancaster's manors ride to Pontefract with their rolls, the year's rents in coin and kind. Bushels of oats, cartloads of wool and fat geese crowd the castle yard. The steward tallies them on notched sticks. The money will pay for archers and for the king's wars in France; the villagers hope only that it is enough to keep the soldiers away.",
  "house-of-lancaster:ritual": "On the night of 20 March 1413, as his father Henry IV lay dead, the new king Henry V went alone to Westminster Abbey and spent the night with a holy recluse in his cell. Chroniclers said he came out a changed man, leaving the wild companions of his youth behind. He was asking God what kind of king he should be, and he never told anyone the answer he received.",
  "house-of-lancaster:rain": "In the summer of 1485 Henry Tudor, the last hope of the Lancastrian cause, waited at Harfleur with a small army of exiles and French soldiers. Everything hung on the wind. On 1 August it turned, and his ships sailed west; on 7 August he landed at Mill Bay near Milford Haven, kneeling to recite a psalm on Welsh soil. Within three weeks he was king.",
  "house-of-lancaster:flood": "In October 1483 the Duke of Buckingham rose against Richard III in favour of Henry Tudor. But days of rain swelled the Severn so high that his army could not cross; the flood drowned cattle and children in their cradles, and was long remembered as Buckingham's Great Water. His men melted away, and he was betrayed and beheaded at Salisbury. Lancastrian hopes waited two more years.",
  "house-of-lancaster:wind": "Also in October 1483, Henry Tudor sailed from Brittany to join the rising. A storm scattered his fleet in the Channel. When his ship at last reached Poole, he saw soldiers on the shore inviting him to land as friends. He suspected a trap, raised sail, and turned back to France. The storm had cost him the rebellion, and possibly saved his life.",
  "house-of-lancaster:fire": "On 13 June 1381, rebels of the Peasants' Revolt broke into the Savoy Palace, the great London house of John of Gaunt, father of the House of Lancaster. They hated him as the power behind the boy king. They smashed his silver, threw his jewels into the Thames, and burned the palace to the ground; a man caught stealing was thrown into the flames by the rebels themselves. Nothing was rebuilt for a century.",
  "house-of-lancaster:thunder": "In 1402 Henry IV led an army into Wales against Owain Glyndŵr. In September a storm of wind, hail and thunder broke over the camp so violently that the king's tent was blown down and a lance nearly killed him. English chroniclers said Glyndŵr had raised the storm by magic. The army retreated, soaked and hungry, and the Lancastrian king learned that Wales would not be won quickly.",
  "house-of-lancaster:birth": "On 6 December 1421 Catherine of Valois gave birth to a son at Windsor. Henry V was away at the siege of Meaux. A later chronicle says he had warned against Windsor: Henry of Monmouth would reign short and win much, Henry of Windsor would reign long and lose all. Within a year the father was dead, and the baby was king of England and France. The saying came true.",
  "house-of-lancaster:death": "Henry V died at Vincennes on 31 August 1422, aged thirty-five. His body was carried slowly across France to Calais, with a painted leather effigy in royal robes lying on the coffin, and torches burning around it at every town. In England crowds lined the road to London. He was buried in Westminster Abbey on 7 November, his helm and saddle hung above the grave.",

  "celts:hunt": "The Greek writer Arrian, around 130 AD, admired how the Celts hunted hare: not with nets, but on horseback behind swift hounds they called vertragi, for the joy of the chase. He records that they set aside a coin for every hare, fox or deer they killed, and once a year spent the hoard on a sacrifice and feast for their goddess of the hunt, with the hounds crowned with flowers.",
  "celts:battle": "At Telamon in 225 BC the Gauls faced Rome between two Roman armies. Polybius wrote that the Gaesatae warriors stood in the front rank naked but for gold torcs and armbands, and that the din of their countless horns and carnyxes, with the whole army shouting, seemed to come from the land itself. The Romans were afraid; yet the Gauls were crushed, and their torcs carried to the Capitol.",
  "celts:gather": "At Lughnasa (Lughnasadh), the start of harvest at the beginning of August, Irish communities climbed hills to pick the first bilberries, cut the first sheaf, and hold games and matchmaking in honour of the god Lugh. Later, Christians kept the climb as Garland Sunday or Reek Sunday, going barefoot up Croagh Patrick (Cruach Phádraig). The first fruits were a sign that hunger would end and the year would turn out well.",
  "celts:ritual": "Early Irish texts describe the tarbfeis, the bull-feast held to learn who should be king of Tara (Teamhair). A white bull was killed, and one man ate his fill of its flesh and drank the broth, then lay down while four druids chanted over him. Whoever he saw in his sleep would be king; had he lied, it was said, his lips would destroy him. The future was dreamed, gravely, on behalf of all.",
  "celts:rain": "Imagined: on a western sea loch of the Highlands (a' Ghàidhealtachd), the boats of a clan lie drawn up on the shingle, ready to row to the islands for a gathering. A grey haar sits over the water and swallows the headland. The piper plays a slow air to pass the time; the old men tell the young ones this is no rain at all. By noon a breeze from the hills tears the mist apart.",
  "celts:flood": "Aristotle and Ephorus both repeated a story about the Celts of the northern coasts: that they feared nothing, and when the great sea-tides came in over their land they took up their weapons and marched into the waves to meet them, until many were drowned. The Greeks called it rashness. To the Celts, it may have been honour: one did not run from anything, not even the sea.",
  "celts:wind": "Adomnán's Life of Saint Columba (Colm Cille), written about 700 on Iona (Ì), tells how the saint's monks, caught by a great storm in the whirlpool of Corryvreckan (Coire Bhreacain) between the islands, were saved when Columba, far away on Iona, knelt and prayed for them. Island people at sea in skin-covered currachs lived close to such storms, and every crossing began with a blessing and ended with thanks.",
  "celts:fire": "Cormac's Glossary (Sanas Cormaic), written in Ireland around 900, says that at Beltaine, the first of May, druids lit two great fires with incantations, and the cattle were driven between them to protect them from disease for the coming year. In the Highlands and Ireland the custom of the May fire lasted into modern times: hearths were put out and relit from the sacred flame, and the summer grazing began.",
  "celts:thunder": "In 335 BC Alexander the Great met Celtic envoys from the Adriatic on the Danube. He asked what they feared most, expecting them to say himself. They answered that they feared nothing, except that the sky might fall on their heads. Their god Taranis rode the thunder with his wheel, and only the heavens breaking open could shake men who were otherwise unafraid.",
  "celts:birth": "Before Columba (Colm Cille) was born in 521 at Gartan in Donegal (Dún na nGall), his mother Eithne dreamed that an angel brought her a cloak of every colour, which then rose into the air and spread over the mountains and the lakes of Ireland and Scotland. The angel told her that her son would lead countless souls to heaven. The child of a noble family would found Iona and carry his faith to the Picts.",
  "celts:death": "Irish wakes (tórramh) lasted through the night with candles, pipes of tobacco, stories and games; then the women raised the caoineadh, the keen, a sung lament of grief and praise. In 1773 Eibhlín Dubh Ní Chonaill found her husband Art Ó Laoghaire shot dead, and her keen for him became one of the great poems of the Irish language, full of love, fury and the blood on her hands.",

  "franks:hunt": "The Frankish king Theudebert I, grandson of Clovis (Chlodowig), loved to hunt the great wild beasts of the forests. The Byzantine historian Agathias says that in 548 a huge wild bull, an aurochs, charged him; a tree branch it struck fell on the king and wounded him, and he died soon afterwards. Hunting the bison and the aurochs was a royal privilege, and a royal danger.",
  "franks:battle": "In October 732, near Tours and Poitiers, the Frankish army of Charles Martel (Karl) stood in a close shield wall against the cavalry of the Umayyad governor Abd al-Rahman. A Mozarabic chronicle of 754 says the men of the north stood 'like a wall of ice', unmoving, and cut down the Arabs with their swords. Abd al-Rahman fell; at night his army withdrew south, and Charles was called the Hammer.",
  "franks:gather": "Around 800 Charlemagne (Karolus Magnus) issued the Capitulare de villis, rules for his royal estates. It ordered each steward to keep good records, to make wine and beer cleanly, to keep bees and fish ponds, and to grow some ninety listed plants: lilies, roses, sage, rue, cucumbers, apples, pears. Imagined behind it is the steward's anxiety, counting the barrels before the emperor's household arrives to eat them.",
  "franks:ritual": "Gregory of Tours records how, around 555, the clergy of Dijon (Divio) sought the future of the rebellious prince Chramn. They placed three books on the altar, the Prophets, the Apostle and the Gospels, prayed that God would show what would happen, and each was opened at random. All three readings warned of ruin. They were not wrong: in 560 Chramn was burned with his family by his own father.",
  "franks:rain": "Each spring, on the Rogation days (Rogationes) before Ascension, Frankish clergy and villagers walked the bounds of their fields behind the cross, chanting litanies and asking God for good weather and an end to frost and drought. The custom spread from Vienne, where Bishop Mamertus began it in the 470s after earthquakes and fires. Then they went home and waited for the sky to answer.",
  "franks:flood": "Gregory of Tours wrote that in 580 such heavy rains fell for twelve days in the Auvergne that the rivers burst their banks and drowned fields, people and cattle; the Loire and the Allier swept away houses and the harvest. He listed it among the signs of an anxious year, with an earthquake, a plague and strange lights in the sky. Floods to him were warnings as well as disasters.",
  "franks:wind": "Imagined: in winter at Aachen (Aquisgrani), the north wind howls through the new palace, rattling the lead roof of the Palatine Chapel. Monks of the palace school bend over their wax tablets by candlelight while servants block the doors with straw. The emperor, Einhard tells us, liked to swim in the hot springs; tonight the steam is torn away by the gale, and the old men talk of the pagan gods of storm.",
  "franks:fire": "Einhard tells how Charlemagne built a great wooden bridge over the Rhine at Mainz (Mogontia), five hundred paces long, which took ten years to complete. In 813, the year before the emperor's death, it caught fire and burned to the waterline in three hours, so that not a single plank was left above the water. His court remembered it later as one of the signs that his end was near.",
  "franks:thunder": "Among the omens before Charlemagne's death in 814, Einhard lists that lightning struck the golden apple on the roof of his church at Aachen and hurled it onto the bishop's house nearby. Eclipses and earthquakes shook the palace, and the inscription naming Charles as the builder faded until it could hardly be read. The emperor himself ignored all of it, Einhard says.",
  "franks:birth": "Gregory of Tours records that Queen Clotilde (Chrodechildis), a Christian, had her first son by the pagan King Clovis baptised; the baby died in his white robes. Clovis blamed her God. When a second son, Chlodomer, was born she baptised him too, and he fell ill. She prayed, and the child lived. The king took notice. Not long afterwards, around 496, Clovis himself was baptised at Reims (Remi).",
  "franks:death": "In 1653 workmen in Tournai (Tornacum) dug up the grave of Childeric I (Childerich), father of Clovis, who died around 481. He lay with his sword, a gold seal ring showing his long hair, a cloak sewn with some three hundred golden bees, and a horse's head; later digs found whole horses buried around him. His people had sent their king to the next world as a warrior, with everything he needed.",

  "french:hunt": "On 14 July 1789, the day the Bastille fell, Louis XVI wrote one word in his diary: 'Rien', nothing. He meant there had been no kill in the hunt that day. Hunting was his passion and his comfort; he recorded every stag and boar for years. The word has become a symbol of a king who did not see his world ending, but it also shows a shy man who loved the forests of Versailles.",
  "french:battle": "On 20 September 1792 the ragged army of the new French Republic (République française) faced the Prussians at Valmy. The cannonade went on for hours; the French did not break, and General Kellermann raised his hat on his sword and cried 'Vive la Nation!' The Prussians withdrew. Goethe, watching from their side, told the officers that from this place and this day began a new era in world history.",
  "french:gather": "French law and custom long gave the poor the right of glanage: after the harvest was carried off, they could walk the stubble and pick up the ears the reapers had left. In 1857 Jean-François Millet painted three women stooping in the field at Chailly, the wealthy harvest piled in the distance. Critics in Paris found it dangerous; the women themselves only hoped for enough bread for the winter.",
  "french:ritual": "In 1555 Michel de Nostradamus published his first Prophecies, and Queen Catherine de' Medici summoned him to the court to cast horoscopes for her children. When Henri II died in 1559 from a lance splinter in a tournament, people read one quatrain as having foretold it. The Catholic Church disapproved of fortune-telling, but the anxious queen and many French readers kept asking about the future.",
  "french:rain": "When drought or endless rain threatened the harvest, Parisians brought out the reliquary of Sainte Geneviève, patroness of the city. In great processions, recorded many times from the Middle Ages to the 1700s, it was carried down from her abbey to Notre-Dame with the shrine of Saint Marcel, barefoot monks and all the guilds following. Then the city waited, praying, for the weather to change.",
  "french:flood": "In January 1910 the Seine rose for days until it stood more than eight metres above normal. Water filled the Métro and the cellars, and Parisians crossed their streets on planks and in rowing boats. People watched the stone Zouave soldier on the Pont de l'Alma: the water reached his shoulders. The city did not panic; cafés stayed open, and neighbours fed each other until the river fell.",
  "french:wind": "On 26 and 27 December 1999 two great storms, Lothar and Martin, swept across France with winds over 170 km/h. Millions lost power at Christmas, and some ninety people died. In the park of Versailles about ten thousand trees fell, including ones planted under Marie-Antoinette. Afterwards people from all over the world sent money to replant the gardens, as if mourning old friends.",
  "french:fire": "On the evening of 15 April 2019 the roof of Notre-Dame de Paris caught fire. Crowds gathered along the Seine and watched the spire collapse into the flames. Many knelt and sang 'Je vous salue Marie' into the night. Firefighters saved the bell towers and the relics. Five years later, on 7 December 2024, the cathedral reopened, its bells ringing again over Paris.",
  "french:thunder": "On 10 May 1752 at Marly-la-Ville, following Benjamin Franklin's idea, Thomas-François Dalibard set up a tall iron rod during a thunderstorm. An old soldier left to watch it saw sparks leap from the rod with a crackle, and ran to fetch the village priest. It was the first proof that lightning is electricity. Thunder, the voice of heaven, had become something the French could study.",
  "french:birth": "Louis XIII and Anne of Austria had been married for twenty-three years without a living child when, on 5 September 1638, a son was born at Saint-Germain-en-Laye. The whole kingdom rang its bells and called him Louis Dieudonné, the gift of God. No one knew that this boy, Louis XIV, would reign for seventy-two years, build Versailles, and make France both glorious and exhausted.",
  "french:death": "On 15 December 1840 the remains of Napoleon, brought back from Saint Helena (Sainte-Hélène), were carried through Paris to the Invalides. The cold was bitter and snow fell, yet hundreds of thousands lined the Champs-Élysées. Old soldiers of the Grande Armée wept in their faded uniforms. He had asked to rest 'on the banks of the Seine, among the French people I loved so much'.",

  "jeanne-darc:hunt": "On 18 June 1429, after Orléans, Jeanne's army was searching for the retreating English near Patay. The English longbowmen lay hidden in a hedge. Then a stag broke from the woods and ran straight into their lines, and the archers raised a hunting cry. The French scouts heard it, the cavalry charged before the stakes were set, and the English army was destroyed. People said God had sent the stag.",
  "jeanne-darc:battle": "On 7 May 1429 the French stormed Les Tourelles, the English fort guarding the bridge of Orléans. Jeanne was struck by an arrow above her breast while setting a ladder, and was carried away in tears. She had the arrow drawn, prayed, and returned with her banner. As the soldiers saw it touch the wall they surged forward, and the fort fell. The next morning the English lifted the siege.",
  "jeanne-darc:gather": "At her trial in Rouen in 1431 the judges asked Jeanne whether she had learned a trade. She said yes: she had learned to sew and spin from her mother, and she feared no woman of Rouen at it. At Domrémy she had helped in the house, led the animals to pasture and worked in the fields at harvest. It was a plain village life she gave up for the war.",
  "jeanne-darc:ritual": "Jeanne told her judges that when she was about thirteen she heard a voice from God in her father's garden at Domrémy, at noon in summer, from the side of the church, with a great light. Later Saint Michael (saint Michel), Saint Catherine (sainte Catherine) and Saint Margaret (sainte Marguerite) spoke to her, often when the bells rang. They told her the future she must make: to raise the siege of Orléans and see the Dauphin crowned.",
  "jeanne-darc:rain": "When Jeanne reached Orléans on 29 April 1429, the supply boats could not sail upstream: the wind blew against them. The commander Dunois testified later that she told him to wait, and that suddenly, as if at her word, the wind changed and the boats sailed with full sails past the English forts. He said from that moment he had hope in her.",
  "jeanne-darc:flood": "Imagined: spring at Domrémy, and the Meuse spills over its meadows after the thaw. The village children are kept from the water, and the men wade out to bring the sheep to higher ground near the Bois Chenu. Jeanne, a girl among them, helps carry lambs. In the evening the village gathers in the little church of Saint Remy (Saint-Rémy) and gives thanks that the water stopped short of the houses.",
  "jeanne-darc:wind": "Imagined: in the winter of 1430, Jeanne is held in the tower of Beaurevoir castle, prisoner of the Burgundians, waiting to be sold to the English. A storm batters the walls and the wind whistles through the shutters. She hears that Compiègne, which she promised to help, is still besieged. Despairing of waiting, she leaps from the tower, and survives, to everyone's astonishment.",
  "jeanne-darc:fire": "On 30 May 1431 Jeanne was burned in the Old Market (Vieux-Marché) at Rouen. She asked for a cross; an English soldier tied two sticks together and gave it to her, and a priest held up the crucifix from the church so she could see it through the smoke. She called the name of Jesus again and again. Witnesses said many wept, and an English secretary cried, 'We have burned a saint.'",
  "jeanne-darc:thunder": "Imagined: on the road to Reims in July 1429 a summer storm breaks over the army, and thunder rolls across the wheatfields of Champagne. Soldiers who have sworn all their lives look at the Maid and fall silent, because she forbids their oaths. Jeanne rides on under the rain with her banner furled. By evening the sky clears over Troyes, and the men say it is a good sign for the coronation.",
  "jeanne-darc:birth": "Jeanne was born around 1412 in Domrémy, a village on the border of Lorraine, to Jacques d'Arc, a farmer, and Isabelle Romée. People in France told an old prophecy, said to be Merlin's: a maid would come from the oak wood of the Lorraine marches to save the kingdom. Her parents could not imagine it was their daughter, and later fiercely opposed her leaving home.",
  "jeanne-darc:death": "After the burning, the English had Jeanne's ashes thrown into the Seine so that nothing would be left to venerate. In 1456 a retrial, begun at the plea of her old mother Isabelle Romée, declared the verdict null. Isabelle stood in Notre-Dame of Paris and asked for justice for her daughter. In 1920, nearly five centuries later, Jeanne was declared a saint.",

  "knights-templar:hunt": "The Templar Rule (Règle du Temple), approved in 1129, forbade the brothers to hunt with hawks or with hounds, or to shout in the chase, for such amusements were worldly. It made one exception: a knight might hunt the lion, 'which goes about seeking whom it may devour', for that was the enemy of God. Their hunting was meant to be war on evil, not sport.",
  "knights-templar:battle": "On 4 July 1187 the army of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, maddened by thirst, was trapped by Saladin at the Horns of Hattin. The Templars charged again and again, but the army was destroyed and the True Cross (Vera Crux) captured. Afterwards Saladin had about two hundred captured Templars and Hospitallers executed. Their Master, Gerard de Ridefort, who had urged the march, was spared.",
  "knights-templar:gather": "The Templars' fighting in the East was paid for by thousands of farms in Europe. At Cressing Temple in Essex their brothers built two great timber barns in the 1200s, still standing, to hold the harvest of wheat and barley. Lay brothers and hired reapers worked the fields; the profit was shipped to Acre. To a farmhand there, the Holy Land was something paid for in sheaves.",
  "knights-templar:ritual": "A man joining the Temple was received at night, in chapter, before the gathered brothers. He was asked whether he had debts, a wife, or illness, and warned that he would give up his own will entirely. He swore obedience, chastity and poverty, received the white mantle with the red cross, and the brothers sang Psalm 133 (Ecce quam bonum). What future God gave him was no longer his to ask.",
  "knights-templar:rain": "Imagined: in the spring of 1250 a ship of Templar recruits waits in the harbour of Marseille for the passage season. Every morning the captain sniffs the air, and every morning the mistral blows the wrong way. The young knights pray in the Templar chapel by the port and polish their mail. When at last the wind turns, they cross themselves and sail for Acre, not knowing they will never return.",
  "knights-templar:flood": "Imagined: in winter the Jordan rises brown and fast at the pilgrims' fords. Templar knights, sworn to protect travellers on the road from Jerusalem to the river, ride down to warn a band of pilgrims not to cross. The pilgrims weep; they came so far to bathe where Christ was baptised. The knights camp beside them and keep watch until the water falls and the crossing is safe.",
  "knights-templar:wind": "In May 1291 the Mamluks broke into Acre, the last great city of the crusaders. The Templars held their fortress by the sea for days while ships took refugees away. A storm-swept sea made the escape deadly; women and children drowned in the harbour. On 28 May the walls of the Temple collapsed, killing defenders and attackers together. The Order's treasure sailed north to Sidon with Tibald Gaudin.",
  "knights-templar:fire": "On 18 March 1314 Jacques de Molay, last Grand Master of the Temple, and Geoffroi de Charney were burned alive on a small island in the Seine, facing Notre-Dame. They had retracted their confessions, declaring the Order innocent. De Molay asked to be tied facing the cathedral with his hands free to pray. The crowd was moved by their calm, and some gathered their ashes as relics.",
  "knights-templar:thunder": "Imagined: in the castle of Safed in Galilee, rebuilt by the Templars in the 1240s, a night thunderstorm breaks over the walls. The brothers rise for matins as the Rule demands, and say their paternosters in the dark chapel while lightning shows the hills below. Not one may leave his place or speak needlessly. When the storm passes, the watch reports the road is quiet.",
  "knights-templar:birth": "The Templars were monks and could have no children; their Rule even forbade them to stand as godfathers. Their own birth came in 1129 at the Council of Troyes, when Hugues de Payens and a handful of poor knights who guarded pilgrims received a Rule and the backing of Bernard of Clairvaux. Few guessed this little brotherhood would grow rich and powerful across all Christendom.",
  "knights-templar:death": "The Templar Rule set out what to do when a brother died. For seven days every brother said a hundred paternosters (Pater noster) for his soul, and for forty days his portion of food and drink was given to a poor man. A dead brother was buried at the house where he died, in his habit. There was no grief displayed; a knight of the Temple went to God with his brothers praying.",

  "burgundians:hunt": "At the park of Hesdin in Artois, the dukes of Burgundy hunted deer in a walled forest, and kept a gallery of mechanical tricks to surprise their guests: statues that squirted water, floors that dropped visitors into sacks of feathers, and a machine that made thunder and rain. Philip the Good (Philippe le Bon) restored it in the 1430s. A hunt there ended in laughter, wet clothes and a feast.",
  "burgundians:battle": "On 2 March 1476 Charles the Bold (Charles le Téméraire)'s army met the Swiss at Grandson. As the Swiss main body came over the hill, the great horns of Uri and Unterwalden, called the Bull and the Cow, bellowed across the valley. The Burgundians panicked and fled, leaving behind the duke's tents, tapestries, gold and diamonds. The Swiss divided the treasure; the duke's pride never recovered.",
  "burgundians:gather": "In 1395 Duke Philip the Bold (Philippe le Hardi) banned the 'disloyal' Gamay grape from the Côte d'Or, ordering only Pinot noir to be planted, and so shaped Burgundy wine forever. In 1443 the chancellor Nicolas Rolin founded the Hôtel-Dieu at Beaune to care for the poor, funded by gifts of vineyards. At harvest the pickers knew their grapes would buy bread and beds for the sick.",
  "burgundians:ritual": "On 17 February 1454 at Lille, Philip the Good held the Feast of the Pheasant (Banquet du Faisan). After the fall of Constantinople, a live pheasant decked in jewels was brought in, and the duke and his knights swore great vows on it to go on crusade, in an atmosphere half chivalric game, half sincere prayer. They asked God for the future of Christendom. The crusade never sailed.",
  "burgundians:rain": "Imagined: autumn in Dijon, and the rain has fallen for nine days. The grape harvest waits on the slopes of the Côte, swelling and splitting. The vignerons gather in the church of Notre-Dame and look at the sky between Masses. At last a north wind comes down from the plateau and dries the hills. Within a day every family is in the rows, cutting.",
  "burgundians:flood": "Imagined: in the Burgundian Low Countries in 1446, a storm tide breaks dikes in Flanders and Zeeland. The duke's officers ride out to count the drowned villages and the lost rents. In Bruges, merchants watch the canals rise and wonder whether their warehouses will survive. When the water recedes, the duke grants tax relief, and the peasants begin again to wall out the sea.",
  "burgundians:wind": "Imagined: in June 1468 Margaret of York crosses from Margate to Sluis to marry Charles the Bold. A summer gale tosses the fleet, and her ladies are sick and frightened. When at last the ships enter the Zwin, the Burgundian court waits with music and jewels. Days of feasting follow at Bruges, with a whale-shaped float and a unicorn. The storm becomes a story told at table.",
  "burgundians:fire": "In 1468 the city of Liège rebelled against the Duke of Burgundy. Charles the Bold took it by storm, and ordered it burned. For weeks his men set fire to street after street, sparing only the churches; citizens were drowned in the Meuse in pairs. The French king Louis XI, forced to watch, said nothing. The proud city remembered the burning for generations.",
  "burgundians:thunder": "Imagined: in summer 1475 a thunderstorm breaks over the Burgundian camp at the siege of Neuss on the Rhine. Lightning strikes near the ducal tents, and the soldiers kneel in the mud. The siege has lasted almost a year. Some say God is against the duke; Charles the Bold orders the cannons fired back at the storm. Within weeks he abandons the siege.",
  "burgundians:birth": "On 13 February 1457 Isabelle of Bourbon gave birth to a daughter in Brussels. Her husband, the future Charles the Bold, had hoped for a son; there would be no other child. Twenty years later, when Charles fell at Nancy, this girl, Mary of Burgundy (Marie de Bourgogne), inherited all his lands. She married Maximilian of Austria, and her grandson became Emperor Charles V.",
  "burgundians:death": "For the tomb of Philip the Bold, who died in 1404, Claus Sluter and his followers carved forty little marble mourners, the pleurants, walking in procession beneath the duke's effigy, their faces hidden by hoods, each grieving differently. They show how the Burgundian court buried its dukes: endless black-clad processions, chants, candles, and grief made into art. They still walk today in Dijon.",

  "dutch:hunt": "From 1619 Dutch whalers summered at Smeerenburg, 'Blubber Town', on an island off Spitsbergen. Men rowed out in open sloops after the Greenland right whale, harpooned it, towed it to shore and boiled the blubber in great copper kettles. Hundreds of men worked there amid smoke and stench under the midnight sun. Many died of cold and scurvy, and their graves still lie on the Arctic shore.",
  "dutch:battle": "In June 1667, during the Second Anglo-Dutch War, Michiel de Ruyter's fleet sailed up the Medway, broke the chain across the river at Chatham and burned the English warships at anchor. They towed home the flagship Royal Charles as a trophy; her carved stern is still in the Rijksmuseum. In London Samuel Pepys feared the end; in the Republic the bells rang for days.",
  "dutch:gather": "In 1612 the Beemster lake north of Amsterdam was pumped dry by forty-three windmills, and the bottom was laid out in a perfect grid of fields, roads and ditches. Merchants who had invested watched the first wheat and cattle on land that had been water. To the Dutch, harvesting the polder was a sober miracle: land made by their own hands, with God's blessing.",
  "dutch:ritual": "Dutch Calvinists did not try to read the future in stars or cards; they prayed. On the Biddag voor Gewas en Arbeid, the Day of Prayer for Crops and Labour on the second Wednesday of March, farmers and fishermen in the Reformed churches asked God to bless the coming season. In Bible Belt villages it is still kept, with school out and the church full, solemn and hopeful.",
  "dutch:rain": "In October 1688 William of Orange's (Willem van Oranje) invasion fleet waited at Hellevoetsluis for an east wind to carry it to England. A first attempt was driven back by storms. On 11 November the wind turned east, the 'Protestant wind', which carried the Dutch ships down the Channel while it kept the English fleet pinned in the Thames. Dutch ministers had prayed for exactly that.",
  "dutch:flood": "On the night of 18 to 19 November 1421, the Saint Elizabeth's flood (Sint-Elisabethsvloed) broke the dikes of the Grote Waard in Holland. Some twenty villages vanished; the land became the Biesbosch marsh. A story says that after the storm a wooden cradle floated ashore with a baby asleep in it, kept balanced by a cat jumping from side to side. The place became Kinderdijk, the children's dike.",
  "dutch:wind": "On the night of 31 January 1953 a northwest storm drove the North Sea against Zeeland and South Holland. At high tide dikes broke in scores of places; 1,836 people drowned, many in their beds. Survivors waited on roofs for days. The Dutch called it the Watersnoodramp, and answered with the Delta Works, vowing that the wind and sea would never take them like that again.",
  "dutch:fire": "On 7 July 1652 Amsterdam's old Town Hall (stadhuis) burned down. Among the crowd watching was the young Jan van der Heyden, who never forgot the helpless bucket chains. In 1672 he and his brother invented the fire engine with a flexible leather hose, and Amsterdam's fires became far less deadly. He later painted the new Town Hall, in sharp quiet detail, as if to make sure it stayed.",
  "dutch:thunder": "On 12 October 1654 a gunpowder store in Delft exploded with a roar heard as far as Texel. Some forty tonnes of powder flattened a quarter of the city and killed over a hundred people, among them Carel Fabritius, painter of the Goldfinch, in his studio. The Delftse Donderslag, the Delft Thunderclap, was taken as God's warning; the city rebuilt, and Vermeer painted its quiet streets.",
  "dutch:birth": "On 14 November 1650, eight days after his father William II died of smallpox, a son was born to Mary Stuart in The Hague (Den Haag). The regents of Holland, glad to be rid of the Orange stadholders, kept the baby from power for years. Many Dutch people still hoped in him. In 1672, the Disaster Year (Rampjaar), he saved the Republic, and in 1689 he became William III (Willem III) of England.",
  "dutch:death": "Admiral Michiel de Ruyter was mortally wounded off Sicily in 1676. His embalmed body was brought home, and on 18 March 1677 he was buried in the Nieuwe Kerk of Amsterdam with a great public funeral. Thousands lined the streets and canals. The Republic honoured him with a marble tomb, but sober Dutch preachers reminded the mourners that he had been a humble, praying man.",

  "spanish:hunt": "Philip IV (Felipe IV) was a passionate hunter, and Velázquez painted him in plain brown hunting clothes with his gun and dog for the hunting lodge of Torre de la Parada near Madrid. Royal hunts were held in great enclosures of canvas, the tela, where deer and boar were driven past the king and court. The montería was spectacle and politics; the beaters only hoped the king shot straight.",
  "spanish:battle": "On 7 October 1571 the Holy League fleet under Don Juan of Austria (Don Juan de Austria) met the Ottoman fleet at Lepanto. A young soldier, Miguel de Cervantes, was ill with fever but insisted on fighting; he was shot twice in the chest and lost the use of his left hand. He later called it 'the most noble occasion that past or present ages have seen', and was proud of it all his life.",
  "spanish:gather": "Each winter the olive pickers (aceituneros) of Jaén spread nets beneath the trees and beat the branches with long poles, gathering the olives for the mills. They were landless day labourers, and the great estates owned the trees. In 1937 the poet Miguel Hernández wrote 'Aceituneros', asking proud Andalusians of Jaén who had raised the olive trees. It became a hymn of the province.",
  "spanish:ritual": "In 1588, while the Armada sailed against England, Philip II (Felipe II) ordered prayers in every church of Spain, and at the Escorial monks prayed in shifts day and night before the Blessed Sacrament. The king himself spent hours kneeling, asking God for victory. When the news of disaster came, he is said to have answered calmly that he had sent his ships to fight men, not the winds.",
  "spanish:rain": "In the dry heart of Castile and Andalusia, when drought lasted too long, towns held rogativas pro pluvia: processions carrying the image of their patron saint or Virgin through the fields, praying for rain. Some images were taken from their shrine and not returned until rain fell. Farmers walked behind in silence, looking up at a white sky, waiting for the world to change.",
  "spanish:flood": "On 14 October 1957 days of rain sent the river Turia surging through Valencia. Water rose over five metres in streets near the old city; at least eighty people died. Neighbours sheltered on upper floors and roofs. The city decided never to let the river through again: it was diverted south, and its old bed became the Jardín del Turia, a long green park through Valencia.",
  "spanish:wind": "In September 1588, after their defeat in the Channel, the ships of the Armada sailed around Scotland and Ireland. Atlantic storms drove some two dozen onto the Irish coast. Captain Francisco de Cuéllar, shipwrecked at Streedagh in County Sligo, survived and wrote of crawling ashore among a thousand corpses, then being hidden by Irish chiefs, before he made his way home to Spain.",
  "spanish:fire": "On Christmas Eve 1734 a fire broke out in the old Alcázar palace in Madrid. It burned for four days. Servants and monks smashed windows and threw paintings out into the snow to save them; Velázquez's Las Meninas was among those rescued, but some five hundred works were lost. Philip V (Felipe V) built the new Royal Palace (Palacio Real) on the site, entirely in stone, so it could not burn again.",
  "spanish:thunder": "Imagined: in summer 1640 a thunderstorm breaks over the plain of La Mancha. In the village of Campo de Criptana the miller locks the sails of his windmill and the church bells ring against the storm, as was the custom, to drive away hail. Farmers kneel among the vines. When the hail spares their grapes, they gather in the church to give thanks to the Cristo of the village.",
  "spanish:birth": "On 6 November 1661 Queen Mariana gave birth to a son in Madrid, days after the death of the king's elder boy. The child, Carlos, was frail, the fruit of generations of royal cousins marrying. All Spain prayed he would live and give an heir. He did reign for thirty-five years as Carlos II, called the Bewitched (el Hechizado), but died childless, and Europe went to war over his throne.",
  "spanish:death": "Spanish kings are buried in the Pantheon of Kings (Panteón de los Reyes) beneath the high altar of the Escorial, built under Philip II. But first the bodies rest for decades in a sealed chamber, the pudridero, until they are reduced to bones. Only then are they placed in the marble urns. It is a severe, patient custom of Spanish Catholic faith: humility in death before the long wait for Judgment.",

  "portuguese:hunt": "From the 1700s until 1987, men of the Azores (Açores) hunted sperm whales from open wooden boats with hand harpoons. A lookout on a hill sent up a rocket when he saw a whale's spout, and men left fields and shops and ran to the boats at Lajes do Pico. It was dangerous and poor work, with prayers to the saints before launching. Today the old lookouts watch whales for tourists.",
  "portuguese:battle": "On 14 August 1385 at Aljubarrota a small Portuguese army under King João I and the constable Nuno Álvares Pereira defeated a much larger Castilian army that had come to claim the throne. The Portuguese dug ditches and pits, and English archers helped them. Legend says the baker woman Brites de Almeida killed seven fleeing Castilians with her bread paddle. Portugal stayed independent.",
  "portuguese:gather": "In the Douro valley the grape harvest for port wine was carried down the steep terraces in heavy baskets by men in single file. At night the grapes were trodden barefoot in stone lagares, the treaders linked arm in arm, marching in rhythm to an accordion, a drum, and the foreman's call. Tired, they sang. It was hard work, but also a festival of the whole year's labour.",
  "portuguese:ritual": "On the night of 7 July 1497 Vasco da Gama and his crews kept vigil in the little chapel at Restelo, by the Tagus (Tejo), praying for their voyage to India. The next morning they walked to the boats in procession, carrying candles, while priests chanted and the crowd wept, many believing they would never see them again. Two years later, fewer than half returned.",
  "portuguese:rain": "King Sebastião vanished at the battle of Alcácer Quibir (Alcácer-Quibir) in Morocco on 4 August 1578, and his body was never clearly found. Portugal soon fell under Spanish rule. For centuries many Portuguese believed that he would return on a foggy morning to save his country. Sebastianism became a feeling of saudade and hope: waiting for the fog to lift and the hidden king to ride out.",
  "portuguese:flood": "On All Saints' Day, 1 November 1755, a great earthquake struck Lisbon (Lisboa) during morning Mass. Survivors ran to the open space of the waterfront, and saw the sea draw back, exposing the riverbed, before a tsunami swept in over the quays and the Praça do Comércio. Thousands drowned. Across Europe, people asked how God could allow this, and Voltaire wrote a poem of anger.",
  "portuguese:wind": "In 1488 Bartolomeu Dias was driven far south by a storm that lasted thirteen days. When it ended, he sailed east and found he had passed the southern tip of Africa. On his way home he saw the cape and named it the Cape of Storms (Cabo das Tormentas). King João II renamed it the Cape of Good Hope (Cabo da Boa Esperança), because it opened the sea road to India. The storm had shown the way.",
  "portuguese:fire": "The 1755 earthquake struck on All Saints' Day, when churches were full of candles. The candles fell, and fires burned across Lisbon for five or six days, consuming palaces, libraries and the Royal Opera. When asked what to do, the Marquis of Pombal is said to have answered, 'Bury the dead and feed the living'. He rebuilt the Baixa in straight streets.",
  "portuguese:thunder": "In Camões's epic Os Lusíadas of 1572, as da Gama's fleet nears the Cape of Good Hope a dark cloud rises, the sea roars, and the giant Adamastor appears, the spirit of the cape. In a voice like thunder he foretells the shipwrecks and deaths that await the Portuguese on this route. The sailors listen in terror, but sail on. Many readers heard their own history in it.",
  "portuguese:birth": "On 4 March 1394, in Porto, Philippa of Lancaster (Filipa de Lencastre) gave birth to her third son, Henrique. The country was newly independent and uncertain. No one knew this prince would be remembered as Henry the Navigator (Infante Dom Henrique), sponsoring voyages down the coast of Africa that changed the world, for good and ill. His mother, it is said, gave each son a sword before her death.",
  "portuguese:death": "Inês de Castro, beloved of Prince Pedro, was killed in 1355 on his father's orders. When Pedro became king, legend says he had her body exhumed, crowned and seated on a throne, and made the court kiss her hand. Their tombs in Alcobaça monastery face each other, so that on the Day of Judgment, when they rise, the first thing each sees will be the other.",

  "italians:hunt": "Lorenzo de' Medici, ruler of Florence (Firenze), wrote a poem around 1470, 'L'uccellagione di starne', about a morning of partridge hawking in the Tuscan hills with his friends. It is full of names of falcons and dogs, quarrels over who flew best, a lost hawk, and a hungry return to dinner. Hunting for the Florentine elite was friendship, poetry and the joy of the countryside together.",
  "italians:battle": "On 29 May 1176 the cities of the Lombard League (Lega Lombarda) faced Emperor Frederick Barbarossa at Legnano. The citizen infantry gathered around the Carroccio, a great ox-cart bearing the city's banner and an altar, and swore to defend it to death. They held, the Milanese cavalry returned, and the emperor was unhorsed and briefly thought dead. The free communes of Italy had won their liberty.",
  "italians:gather": "In 1338 Ambrogio Lorenzetti painted the Effects of Good Government (Effetti del Buon Governo) on the walls of Siena's Palazzo Pubblico. In the countryside outside the city gate, peasants reap wheat, thresh it, drive mules loaded with sacks to town, and hunt with hawks, while a winged figure of Security watches over all. The council of the Nine wanted citizens to remember that peace brings the harvest. Ten years later the plague came.",
  "italians:ritual": "When a pope dies, the cardinals are locked in conclave to choose a successor, praying to the Holy Spirit. Outside, crowds in Saint Peter's Square (Piazza San Pietro) watch a little chimney on the Sistine Chapel (Cappella Sistina). Black smoke means no decision; white smoke and the bells mean a new pope. People wait, many in prayer, not knowing who will lead the Church into the next years.",
  "italians:rain": "Imagined: November in the Po valley (Pianura Padana), and the fog is so thick in Mantua (Mantova) that the boatmen will not take their barges out onto the lakes. Merchants waiting to send cheese and rice downriver to Venice sit in the taverns and complain. The innkeeper says the nebbia will lift when it wants to. On the third morning the sun burns through, and the river is suddenly crowded with sails.",
  "italians:flood": "On 4 November 1966 the Arno burst its banks in Florence (Firenze). Water and mud up to six metres deep filled the streets around Santa Croce, damaging Cimabue's crucifix and millions of books. Young volunteers from Italy and around the world came to dig out the libraries and archives by hand; Florentines called them the angeli del fango, the mud angels.",
  "italians:wind": "On 25 November 1343 Petrarch (Francesco Petrarca) was in Naples (Napoli) when a great storm struck the coast. He wrote to a friend that he saw the sea rise over the harbour, ships wrecked against each other, and the young queen walking barefoot to church with her ladies to pray. He vowed he would never again travel by sea if he could help it. His letter is one of the earliest eyewitness storm descriptions in Europe.",
  "italians:fire": "On 7 February 1497, carnival day in Florence, the followers of the Dominican friar Girolamo Savonarola built a huge pyramid in the Piazza della Signoria and burned 'vanities' (falò delle vanità): mirrors, cosmetics, playing cards, lutes, books and paintings. Children had collected them door to door. Many Florentines felt pure and saved; others were furious. Fifteen months later Savonarola himself was burned in the same square.",
  "italians:thunder": "On the night of 5 April 1492 lightning struck the lantern of the dome of Florence cathedral (Santa Maria del Fiore), and marble blocks fell toward the Medici palace. Lorenzo de' Medici, gravely ill at Careggi, asked which side had fallen, and on hearing the answer said he would die. He died three days later. Florentines took the lightning as an omen of the troubles soon to follow.",
  "italians:birth": "On 15 April 1452 Antonio da Vinci, a notary, wrote in his notebook that his grandson had been born that night at the third hour, and listed the godparents at the baptism. The child was illegitimate, son of the notary Ser Piero and a young woman named Caterina, and could not follow his father's profession. His name was Leonardo. No one in Vinci imagined what he would become.",
  "italians:death": "Raphael (Raffaello Sanzio) died in Rome (Roma) on Good Friday, 6 April 1520, his thirty-seventh birthday. His unfinished painting of the Transfiguration was placed at the head of his bier, and Vasari wrote that all who saw it there wept. He was buried in the Pantheon, as he had asked. Pope Leo X (papa Leone X) wept too. Rome mourned him as if an angel had departed.",

  "sicilians:hunt": "Emperor Frederick II (Federico II), king of Sicily, wrote the treatise 'De arte venandi cum avibus', The Art of Hunting with Birds, in the 1240s. He studied the falcons himself, testing ideas he had read in Aristotle, and records that he had the eyes of vultures sealed to see whether they find food by sight or smell. To him hunting was a science, and he loved his birds more than his courtiers did.",
  "sicilians:battle": "At Vespers (Vespri siciliani) on Easter Monday, 30 March 1282, outside the church of Santo Spirito in Palermo (Palermu), a French soldier insulted a Sicilian woman, and her husband killed him. The crowd rose against the French rulers of Charles of Anjou. In the following weeks the French were killed throughout Sicily. Legend says anyone who could not pronounce 'ciciri', chickpeas, in the Sicilian way was taken for French.",
  "sicilians:gather": "Each spring at the tonnara of Favignana, the fishermen spread a maze of nets to trap the bluefin tuna migrating past. When enough fish were in the final chamber, the rais, the chief, gave the order, and the men chanted the cialoma as they hauled up the net and gaffed the great fish in the mattanza. It was hard, bloody, ancient work, and the sea's harvest for the whole island.",
  "sicilians:ritual": "In 1624, during a plague in Palermo, a woman dreamed that Saint Rosalia (Santa Rusulia) showed her where her bones lay in a cave on Monte Pellegrino. The bones were found and carried through the city in procession, and the plague ended. Every July since, Palermo holds the Festino of Santa Rosalia, pulling her huge chariot through the streets, asking her to protect the city in the year to come.",
  "sicilians:rain": "Sicilians tell that in the Middle Ages a terrible drought struck the island, and the people prayed to Saint Joseph (San Giuseppi) for rain, promising to honour him if it came. The rain fell, and in thanks they set tables of food for the poor. Every 19 March since, families build the Tavola di San Giuseppe, an altar of breads, fava beans and sweets, and feed whoever comes to the door.",
  "sicilians:flood": "At dawn on 28 December 1908 a great earthquake struck the Strait of Messina (Strittu di Missina), and minutes later a tsunami up to twelve metres high swept over the waterfronts of Messina and Reggio. Perhaps eighty thousand people died. Sailors of the Russian navy, anchored nearby, were among the first to dig out survivors. Messina was rebuilt in low houses, and has never forgotten.",
  "sicilians:wind": "Imagined: on the island of Lipari, a fisherman's wife watches the sea go white under a mistral. Her husband's boat is out. The women gather at the church of the Annunziata and pray to Saint Bartholomew (San Bartulumeu), the island's patron. Toward evening a lamp shows on the sea, then a hull. She runs down to the harbour, scolding him all the way, laughing and crying at once.",
  "sicilians:fire": "In March 1669 Etna (Mungibeddu) erupted, and a river of lava flowed toward Catania. Diego Pappalardo led fifty men in wet cowhides to break through the hardened crust and divert the flow. It turned toward the town of Paternò, whose armed men drove them off. The lava reached the city walls of Catania and poured into the sea. Sicilians kept praying to Saint Agatha (Sant'Àita), whose veil was carried against the fire.",
  "sicilians:thunder": "On 11 January 1693 a great earthquake struck the Val di Noto in southeast Sicily, with a roar that survivors compared to thunder from under the earth. Some sixty thousand died, and Catania, Noto, Ragusa and Modica were ruined. The Sicilians rebuilt them in a new, joyful baroque style, golden stone and curving balconies, as if to answer death with beauty.",
  "sicilians:birth": "Constance of Hauteville (Costanza d'Altavilla), heir of the Norman kings of Sicily, became pregnant at forty after nine childless years of marriage to Emperor Henry VI. Fearing doubt, she gave birth on 26 December 1194 in a tent in the public square of Jesi, with townswomen as witnesses. The child, Frederick, grew up an orphan in Palermo and became the emperor called the wonder of the world.",
  "sicilians:death": "Beneath the Capuchin convent of Palermo lie the catacombs (Catacombe dei Cappuccini), where from 1599 friars, then nobles and merchants, were dried and preserved, dressed in their best clothes, and placed in long rows along the walls. Families visited them, prayed, and dressed them again. The last was Rosalia Lombardo, a little girl of two who died in 1920 and still seems to be sleeping.",

  "maltese:hunt": "In 1530 Emperor Charles V granted Malta to the Knights of St John (il-Kavallieri ta' San Ġwann), for a yearly tribute of a single falcon, to be sent to the viceroy of Sicily on All Souls' Day. Each year a trained bird left Malta, the only rent the Knights paid for their island. Falcons, quail and turtle doves passing over in spring and autumn had always been hunted by the Maltese.",
  "maltese:battle": "From May to September 1565 the Ottoman army besieged Malta. Fort St Elmo (Forti Sant'Iermu) held for a month and fell on 23 June; its defenders were killed to the last man. But Birgu and Senglea (l-Isla), defended by the Knights and the Maltese under Grand Master Jean de Valette, never fell. On 8 September the Turks withdrew. The Maltese still celebrate it as the Day of Victory, Il-Vittorja.",
  "maltese:gather": "Along the coast of Gozo (Għawdex) at Xwejni, salt pans cut into the limestone have been worked for centuries. In the hot summer months families flood them with seawater and let the sun dry it; then they sweep the white salt into heaps and carry it in sacks. It is quiet, back-breaking work done the same way by grandparents and grandchildren, under the same sun.",
  "maltese:ritual": "In 1883 Karmni Grima, a woman of Għarb in Gozo, heard a woman's voice calling her as she passed the little chapel of Ta' Pinu. She went in to pray, and the voice asked her to say three Hail Marys (tliet Sliema għalik Marija). Later her own mother was healed after her prayers. Pilgrims have since come to Ta' Pinu to ask the Virgin about their future and leave thanks for answered prayers.",
  "maltese:rain": "Imagined: in a dry spring in the 1700s, the farmers of the Maltese countryside look at their cisterns, half empty, and at the stony terraces. They walk in procession to the parish church with the statue of the patron saint (il-qaddis patrun), praying for rain. For days the sky is blue and cloudless. Then a grey sky comes up from the southwest, and the first heavy drops dry on the warm stones.",
  "maltese:flood": "Imagined: an autumn cloudburst over Malta sends torrents down the narrow valleys, the widien, that cut through the island. In Birkirkara and Qormi, streets become rivers, and the water carries off carts and doors to the harbour. Neighbours pull each other up onto the flat roofs and wait. When the water passes, they sweep out the mud and say it rains in Malta only to make up for the summer.",
  "maltese:wind": "According to the Book of Acts, around AD 60 a ship carrying Saint Paul (San Pawl) as a prisoner to Rome was caught for fourteen days by a violent northeast wind and wrecked on Malta. 'The barbarous people showed us no little kindness,' Luke writes, 'for they kindled a fire because of the rain and the cold.' Maltese people honour Paul's shipwreck on 10 February, as the arrival of their faith.",
  "maltese:fire": "In spring 1942 Malta was the most bombed place on earth. On 7 April German bombs destroyed the Royal Opera House in Valletta (il-Belt Valletta); fires burned in the ruins of the city. People slept in rock shelters and queued for food. On 15 April King George VI awarded the whole island the George Cross for its courage. The ruins of the opera house stood for decades, a reminder.",
  "maltese:thunder": "On 9 April 1942, during an air raid, a German bomb fell through the dome of the Rotunda of Mosta (ir-Rotunda tal-Mosta), where some three hundred people were gathered for evening prayers. It bounced across the floor and did not explode. Nobody was hurt. The people of Mosta called it a miracle of the Virgin, and a replica of the bomb is still kept in the church.",
  "maltese:birth": "On a Maltese child's first birthday the family holds the quċċija. The baby sits on the floor surrounded by objects: a rosary, a pen, a book, a coin, a hard-boiled egg, scissors. Whatever the child picks up first is said to show their future: a priest, a writer, a rich man. The family laughs and cheers, but everyone also wonders quietly what life this child will make.",
  "maltese:death": "Jean de Valette, Grand Master of the Knights and hero of the Great Siege (l-Assedju l-Kbir), died on 21 August 1568 after a stroke while hunting. His body was carried by galley across the harbour to the new city that bore his name, Valletta, and buried in a chapel, later moved to the crypt of St John's Co-Cathedral (il-Konkatidral ta' San Ġwann). The Maltese and the Knights mourned him as the saviour of the island.",

  "britons:siege": "From 1461 to 1468 a small garrison under Dafydd ab Ieuan ab Einion held Harlech Castle (Castell Harlech) on its rock above the sea, the last stronghold of its side in the long civil wars. Supplies came in by the sea gate when they came at all. The men grew thin, and still they would not open the gate. When they finally marched out in August 1468, the story of their stubborn holding out passed into the song 'Men of Harlech' ('Rhyfelgyrch Gwŷr Harlech').",
  "britons:triumph": "Imagined: the war band rides back down the valley to the llys of its lord, horses muddy, two men missing. Women run out along the track to find their own faces among the riders. That night the mead horn goes round the hall, and the pencerdd, the chief bard, stands by the fire to sing each man's deeds by name. The living are praised, the dead are praised louder.",
  "britons:build": "On 26 November 1805 the Pontcysyllte Aqueduct over the Dee valley (Dyffryn Dyfrdwy) was opened with a procession of boats, a band, and cannon fire from the hillside while some eight thousand people watched. Thomas Telford and William Jessop had raised its iron trough on stone piers almost forty metres high. Local masons and labourers had worked on it for ten years, and only one man had been killed.",
  "britons:voyage": "On 28 May 1865 the tea clipper Mimosa left Liverpool with some one hundred and fifty Welsh men, women and children, quarrymen, colliers and shoemakers seeking a place where their language could live. Two children died on the way and two were born. On 28 July they sighted the bare shore of Patagonia (Y Wladfa) and came ashore at Porth Madryn, singing hymns on the beach before they knew what the dry land would ask of them.",
  "britons:festival": "At Christmas 1176 Rhys ap Gruffudd, the Lord Rhys (yr Arglwydd Rhys), held a great gathering in his new castle at Cardigan (Aberteifi), announced, the chronicle says, a year in advance throughout Wales and beyond. Poets competed against poets and harpers, crwth players and pipers against each other, and two chairs were given as prizes, one to a bard of Gwynedd and one to a young harper of the Lord Rhys's own household. It is remembered as the first eisteddfod.",
  "britons:games": "George Owen of Henllys described cnapan in 1603: on holy days whole parishes of Pembrokeshire (Sir Benfro) met on open ground, sometimes two thousand men, some on horseback. A wooden ball boiled in tallow so it slipped from the hand was thrown up, and each side fought to carry it home to its own parish, across fields, hedges and streams, until dark. Men came back bruised and proud, and the story was told all winter.",
  "britons:drought": "Imagined: in a dry summer the streams of Llŷn fall to a trickle and the hay on the hillsides turns brown before it can be cut. The women walk out to the holy well of Saint Cybi (Cybi Sant) with jugs and pins, drop a pin in the water, and ask the saint for rain. In the chapel the minister prays for it by name. Farmers watch the west for cloud over the sea.",
  "britons:quake": "On the morning of 19 July 1984 the ground shook under the Llŷn peninsula. Plates rattled off dressers in Caernarfon and Pwllheli, chimneys cracked, and people in Liverpool and Dublin felt it too. It was among the strongest earthquakes Britain had known for a century, yet no one died. In the farmhouses people talked for weeks about where they had been standing, and the old said the mountain had turned over in its sleep.",
  "britons:winter": "In the winter of 1947 snow fell on Wales (Cymru) from late January and kept falling until March. Drifts buried hedges and lanes, and on the hill farms the sheep lay smothered in their thousands; men dug through the drifts with long poles, feeling for the breathing hollows. Villages were cut off for weeks. When the thaw came, shepherds walked the slopes counting what was left, and many farms had to start again.",
  "britons:plague": "When the Black Death (y Pla Du) reached Wales in 1349, the poet Ieuan Gethin lost his children to it and wrote an elegy. He called the plague a shilling-headed death that came like black smoke, and described the boils, the small seeds, the apples of pain under the arm. He wept that he had to bury his little ones himself. It is one of the few voices that tells us, in Welsh, what that year felt like.",
  "britons:wedding": "In old Wales a wedding began with the gwahoddwr, the bidder, who walked from farm to farm with a staff hung with ribbons, reciting a rhymed invitation and asking each household to bring a gift. On the morning, the groom's friends came to fetch the bride and found the door barred; verses were exchanged through it in a contest called pwnco. Sometimes the bride then fled on horseback and had to be chased.",

  "english:siege": "From June to August 1648 the town of Colchester was besieged during the civil wars. As the weeks passed the townspeople ate horses, then dogs and cats, then candles and soap. Women and children went out to beg the besiegers to let them pass and were turned back to the gates. On 28 August the town surrendered. Its cloth trade was ruined, and the scars of cannon shot can still be seen on its walls.",
  "english:triumph": "On 23 November 1415 Henry V rode back into London after Agincourt. The city had built pageants along his route: giants on London Bridge, a castle with singing maidens, boys dressed as angels throwing gold leaves, and wine running in the conduits. The king himself wore plain purple and would not let his battered helmet be carried before him, saying the glory was God's. Choirs sang 'Deo gratias Anglia' in the streets.",
  "english:build": "On 28 April 1220 Bishop Richard Poore laid the first stones of a new cathedral in the water meadows below Old Sarum, one for the Pope, one for the archbishop, one for himself. Masons, carpenters and carters worked for thirty-eight years, so that Salisbury Cathedral was built in a single style, light and grey and calm. The great spire came later, still the tallest in England, and still standing on its marshy ground.",
  "english:voyage": "In September 1620 the Mayflower left Plymouth with about a hundred passengers, many of them families seeking to worship in their own way. Sixty-six days of autumn gales followed; a main beam cracked and was jacked back into place, and a young man washed overboard caught a halyard and was hauled back. On 9 November they saw the sandy hills of Cape Cod. William Bradford wrote that they fell on their knees and blessed God.",
  "english:festival": "On May morning young people of English villages went out before dawn to the woods to 'bring in the May', returning with green boughs and hawthorn blossom. The tallest tree was cut for a maypole. Philip Stubbes complained in 1583 that it was drawn home by twenty or forty yoke of oxen, each with flowers on its horns, then raised and danced around all day with pipes and drums. The Puritans disapproved; the villagers danced anyway.",
  "english:games": "In medieval England football was a game of whole towns. On Shrove Tuesday the men of one end of the town tried to carry a leather ball to the far end, through streets, rivers and churchyards, with no limit on numbers. Kings banned it again and again, from Edward II in 1314 onwards, for the noise and broken bones. At Ashbourne in Derbyshire the game is still played every Shrovetide, two days long.",
  "english:drought": "In the long summer of 1976 England had almost no rain from May to August. Lawns turned brown, reservoirs shrank to cracked mud, and in parts of Yorkshire and Wales families queued at standpipes in the street with buckets. People were urged to share bathwater and put a brick in the cistern. A minister was appointed for the drought at the end of August; within days the rain came, and kept coming all autumn.",
  "english:quake": "On the evening of 6 April 1580, Wednesday in Easter week, an earthquake shook London and Kent. The great bell of Westminster rang by itself, chimneys toppled, and at Christ Church in Newgate stones fell from the roof onto the congregation, killing two young apprentices, Thomas Grey and Mabel Everet. Pamphlets and ballads appeared within days, calling it a warning from God to repent. Many simply went to church more often that spring.",
  "english:winter": "In the winter of 1683 to 1684 the Thames froze solid for two months. John Evelyn wrote of a street of booths across the ice, coaches plying from Westminster to the Temple as on dry land, bull-baiting, horse races, puppet plays and cooks roasting meat. Printers set up presses on the river and sold cards with your name printed 'on the Thames'. Yet he also wrote of birds dying and the poor without fuel.",
  "english:plague": "In September 1665 plague reached the Derbyshire village of Eyam in a bundle of cloth from London. The rector William Mompesson and the former minister Thomas Stanley persuaded the villagers to shut themselves in so that the sickness would not spread. Food was left at boundary stones, and coins were left in vinegar to pay for it. Some two hundred and sixty died, among them Mompesson's wife Catherine; the neighbouring villages were spared.",
  "english:wedding": "In medieval England the vows were spoken at the church door before the couple went in for the mass; Chaucer's Wife of Bath boasts of five husbands at church door. The banns had been read three Sundays before. Afterwards came the bride ale, brewed by the bride's family and sold to the guests to help the couple start. In some places a cake was broken over the bride's head for luck, and the crumbs were scrambled for.",

  "house-of-lancaster:siege": "From July 1418 to January 1419 Henry V's army besieged Rouen. As food ran out, the city put some twelve thousand of its poor out of the gates, and the besiegers would not let them through. They lived in the ditch between the walls and the camp, eating grass, while babies were born there and hauled up in baskets to be baptized. On Christmas Day the king sent them bread. John Page, an English soldier, wrote it all down in verse.",
  "house-of-lancaster:triumph": "Imagined: the archers come home to Lancashire from the French wars in late summer, sunburnt and thinner, with a bundle of pay and a story apiece. The bells of the parish church ring them in. Their wives and mothers meet them at the bridge, and the alewife opens a fresh barrel. That evening at the inn a piper plays, and the men show the children how far a longbow can throw an arrow.",
  "house-of-lancaster:build": "In 1446 Henry VI laid the first stone of the chapel of his new King's College at Cambridge, planning it to be the finest in the realm. Work stopped through the wars and started again under later kings. Finally, from 1508 to 1515, master mason John Wastell raised the great fan vault, a ceiling of stone like lace. Generations of masons had worked on a building that no one of them saw begun and finished.",
  "house-of-lancaster:voyage": "In May 1497 John Cabot, a Venetian living in Bristol, sailed west in the little ship Matthew with about eighteen men, licensed by Henry VII to seek new lands. After some five weeks, on 24 June, they sighted land, probably Newfoundland. They went ashore briefly, saw signs of people, and turned home. The Bristol fishermen learned of seas so full of cod that, it was said, the fish could be scooped up in baskets.",
  "house-of-lancaster:festival": "In June 1520, near Calais, Henry VIII and Francis I of France met for eighteen days of feasting in a field later called the Field of the Cloth of Gold. A temporary palace of timber and painted cloth was built, a fountain ran red wine for all comers, and the chapels of both kings sang Mass together. There were jousts, wrestling and banquets. Ordinary people came from miles around to stare, drink and dance.",
  "house-of-lancaster:games": "In February 1511 Henry VIII held a great tournament at Westminster to celebrate the birth of a son, Prince Henry. The king himself jousted under the name Sir Loyal Heart. The painted Westminster Tournament Roll shows the procession of riders and the royal trumpeters, among them John Blanke, a Black trumpeter in a yellow turban. Within days the baby prince died, and the court turned from the lists to mourning.",
  "house-of-lancaster:drought": "In 1540 a great drought struck England and much of Europe. Hardly any rain fell from spring to autumn; the Thames shrank so low that the sea came upriver past London Bridge and the water turned salt. Wells dried up, cattle died, and fevers spread with the bad water. Yet the vines ripened early and sweet, and that autumn the wine was remembered as one of the strongest anyone had tasted.",
  "house-of-lancaster:quake": "On 21 May 1382, as a council of churchmen met at Blackfriars in London, the ground shook. The Dover Straits earthquake cracked church towers in Kent and threw down pinnacles at Canterbury. The council had gathered to judge the teachings of John Wycliffe; both sides took the shaking as a sign from God, though they could not agree what it meant. It became known as the Earthquake Council.",
  "house-of-lancaster:winter": "The winter of 1407 to 1408 was remembered for generations as the Great Frost. Chroniclers wrote that it lasted from December to March, that the Thames froze, and that thrushes and blackbirds fell dead from the hedges. Cattle starved in the byres for lack of hay. In the reign of the Lancastrian king Henry IV, people huddled around peat fires and told each other that no one living had seen such cold.",
  "house-of-lancaster:plague": "In August 1485 a new sickness appeared in London: the sweating sickness. It began with a chill and a racing heart, then a drenching sweat, and could kill in a few hours. People said a man could be merry at dinner and dead at supper. It came back in waves, and in 1528 it reached the royal court itself; Henry VIII moved restlessly from house to house to escape it.",
  "house-of-lancaster:wedding": "On 18 January 1486, at Westminster, Henry VII married Elizabeth of York, joining two families that had fought for thirty years. Their badge became the Tudor rose, a red rose with a white rose inside it, carved and painted everywhere from chapel ceilings to children's toys. Bonfires were lit in London streets and wine was given out. For many people the rose meant simply that the long fighting might be over.",

  "celts:siege": "In 52 BC Vercingetorix (Vercingetorixs on his coins) and some eighty thousand Gauls were shut inside the hill town of Alesia, ringed by Caesar's double line of walls and ditches. As food ran low, the Mandubii, whose town it was, sent out their women, children and old people, hoping they would be let through. The Romans refused them. They waited between the walls, starving in sight of both armies, while the defenders watched for the great relief army on the horizon.",
  "celts:triumph": "Posidonius, who travelled in Gaul around 100 BC, wrote that warriors rode home from victory singing a paean, with their spoils and the heads of their enemies hung at their horses' necks. Then came the feast. The bravest man was given the champion's portion, the thigh of the pig, and might have to fight for it. Bards stood up with lyres to praise the deeds of the day, and the hall roared after every verse.",
  "celts:build": "Caesar described the walls of the Gaulish towns in detail, for they baffled his engineers: long timber beams laid in rows across the wall and nailed together, the gaps packed with earth and faced with great stones. The stone kept off fire, the timber kept off the battering ram. Whole communities raised such ramparts at Bibracte and other oppida, and archaeologists still find the iron nails, a forearm long, in their rubble.",
  "celts:voyage": "In 278 BC some twenty thousand Gauls, only half of them fighters and the rest women, children and old people with their wagons, crossed the strait from Europe into Asia, invited as allies by a king of Bithynia. They wandered for years before settling on the high plains of central Anatolia. There they kept their language for centuries; Saint Jerome wrote around 390 AD that the Galatians spoke much as the Treveri of the Rhineland did.",
  "celts:festival": "Samhain (Old Irish Samain), the first of November, began the Irish year. For three days the kings and people gathered at Tara (Teamhair) and other royal sites to feast, hold assemblies and settle laws. On Samhain night, the tales say, the doors of the sídhe, the hills of the otherworld, stood open, and the dead and the fairy folk walked abroad. Fires were lit on the hill of Tlachtga, and every hearth was kindled again from them.",
  "celts:games": "Irish tradition tells of the Óenach Tailten, held every year at Lughnasa in honour of Tailtiu, foster mother of the god Lug, who died clearing the plains for farming. There were horse races, running, spear-throwing and hurling (iománaíocht), poets and storytellers, and a fair of trade. In the tales, the boy Cú Chulainn arrives at Emain Macha driving a ball with his hurley and beats a hundred and fifty boys alone.",
  "celts:drought": "Imagined: the summer sun has burned the grass brown on the plains of Gaul, and the spring at the sacred grove is barely a trickle. The people walk there with offerings, little bronze wheels, carved wooden figures of the sick, a calf. The druid pours milk into the spring and speaks to the goddess of the water by name. The farmers stand silent, eyes on the sky in the west.",
  "celts:quake": "Imagined: the ground shakes one night under a hill town of the Gauls. Pots fall, cattle bellow in their pens, and part of the rampart slides into the ditch. In the morning the people gather by the shrine, frightened and talking all at once, and the druid says the earth mother has turned in her sleep. They bury an offering of bronze in a pit, and the men begin rebuilding the wall before the harvest.",
  "celts:winter": "Imagined: midwinter in a roundhouse in the west. The cattle are in the byre beside the house, and their warmth comes through the wattle wall. Snow lies on the thatch, smoke drifts out through it, and the stores of grain and smoked meat are counted again. In the long dark the storyteller tells the tale of Cú Chulainn's boyhood, and everyone waits for Imbolc (Imbolg) and the first milk of the ewes.",
  "celts:plague": "The Irish annals record that in 664 a great plague called the buide Conaill, the yellow plague, reached Ireland (Éire) on the first of August. It took kings, abbots and the common people alike; Bede wrote that it emptied whole districts of Britain and Ireland. Monks fled to small islands, and many prayed to be spared. Some said the sickness came because there were too many people for the land to feed.",
  "celts:wedding": "Caesar noted a Gaulish custom: when a man married, he set aside from his own goods as much as his bride brought as her dowry. The two sums were kept together and the income saved, and whichever of them outlived the other received both shares with all the profits of the years. In Ireland, marriages at the Lughnasa (Lughnasadh) fair could be trial marriages for a year and a day, ended by simply walking apart.",

  "franks:siege": "In November 885 hundreds of Viking ships came up the Seine and laid siege to Paris (Parisius), then an island city with two fortified bridges. Bishop Gozlin and Count Odo held the walls with the townspeople. When the northern bridge tower was cut off by a flood, twelve defenders fought on until they were killed. The siege lasted almost a year. Abbo, a monk of Saint-Germain, wrote it all in Latin verse (Bella Parisiacae urbis).",
  "franks:triumph": "In 796 the Franks broke into the Ring, the great fortified camp of the Avars on the Hungarian plain, and found the treasure the Avars had gathered over two centuries. Einhard wrote that it was brought to Aachen (Aquisgranum) in fifteen wagons, each drawn by four oxen. Charlemagne gave much of it away to churches, to the pope and to his followers. The Franks said they had never been so rich.",
  "franks:build": "Around 796 Charlemagne began his chapel at Aachen, an eight-sided church of stone with a gallery and a dome, unlike anything north of the Alps. Its architect was Odo of Metz (Odo Mettensis). Columns of marble and porphyry were brought from Rome and Ravenna by cart and boat over the Alps. The chapel was consecrated in 805, and Charlemagne's throne of plain stone still stands in its gallery.",
  "franks:voyage": "In 797 Charlemagne sent an embassy to the caliph Harun al-Rashid in Baghdad. Of the three envoys, only one, a Jewish man named Isaac, came back, bringing a gift: an elephant called Abul-Abbas. He crossed the sea from Africa to Italy, waited out the winter, then crossed the Alps. On 20 July 802 he arrived at Aachen, and the Franks came from all around to see him.",
  "franks:festival": "Gregory of Tours tells how Clovis, king of the Franks, was baptized at Reims, at Christmas, perhaps in 496 or a few years later. The streets were hung with painted cloths, the church decked with white curtains, and the baptistery filled with candles and sweet incense, so that the Franks believed they stood in the scents of paradise. With him, Gregory says, three thousand of his warriors were baptized too.",
  "franks:games": "In 842 at Worms (Wormatia), two Frankish kings held games for their armies. Nithard, who was there, wrote that equal bands of Saxons, Gascons, Austrasians and Bretons rode at each other at full gallop as if in battle, then wheeled away, covering their backs with their shields, and turned to chase each other in turn. The kings themselves joined in at the end, with all the young men. Nobody, he says, was hurt.",
  "franks:drought": "In 792 and 793 a terrible famine struck the Frankish lands, and annals report that in places people were driven to eat meat in Lent. The next year, at the synod of Frankfurt (Franconofurd), Charlemagne fixed the price of grain and bread so that the poor would not be starved by sellers, and ordered his own estates to sell at that price. He told the churches to pray and give alms as well.",
  "franks:quake": "The Royal Frankish Annals record that on 30 April 801, at the second hour of the night, a great earthquake struck, felt across Italy; the roof of the basilica of Saint Paul at Rome fell in. Charlemagne was then travelling home from Rome. In the same year, the annals say, earthquakes shook places along the Rhine, and a sickness followed. Frankish monks wrote such things down carefully, as signs to be understood.",
  "franks:winter": "The Frankish annals remember the winter of 763 to 764 as one of the hardest ever known. Snow and frost lasted from the start of October until spring. Rivers froze so that carts crossed on them, olive trees and vines died in the south, and in the east, travellers said, even parts of the Black Sea had frozen. In the villages, the farmers counted the dead cattle and waited for the thaw.",
  "franks:plague": "Gregory of Tours describes the plague that came to Clermont (Arverna) in 571, the illness that caused swellings in the groin. On one Sunday, he wrote, three hundred bodies lay in the basilica of Saint Peter. Coffins ran out, and ten or more were buried in one pit. Bishop Gall led the people in processions of chanting and fasting, walking to the churches of the saints and praying for the town to be spared.",
  "franks:wedding": "Among the Franks a man paid a bride price to the woman's family for her hand, and on the morning after the wedding night he gave his wife a morning gift, the Morgengabe, which became her own property. Gregory of Tours mentions the morning gifts of queens: whole towns in southern Gaul given to a royal bride. For ordinary families, the gift might be a cow, a cloak or a share of land.",

  "french:siege": "From September 1870 to January 1871 Paris was surrounded and cut off. Letters left by balloon from Montmartre, and pigeons carried replies back on tiny films. As food ran out Parisians ate horses, then cats, dogs and rats; at Christmas the restaurant Voisin served elephant consommé, from Castor and Pollux of the Jardin des Plantes. Women queued all night for bread in the freezing cold, and the people held on through the winter.",
  "french:triumph": "In the summer of 1214 Philip Augustus (Philippe Auguste) came home from victory at Bouvines. The chronicler Guillaume le Breton wrote that along the whole road, peasants and harvesters left their sheaves to stare, villages rang their bells, and churches were hung with tapestries and green branches. In Paris, the students of the schools sang and danced for seven days and nights, and the city could not sleep for joy.",
  "french:build": "In 1144 Abbot Haimon (abbé Haimon) wrote of a wonder at Chartres: men and women of every rank harnessing themselves to carts loaded with stone, wood and grain for the building of the cathedral, a thousand people pulling in silence, or singing hymns, confessing their sins, and stopping only to pray. When the cathedral burned in 1194 they built again, and within a few decades raised the great church that still stands.",
  "french:voyage": "On 20 April 1534 Jacques Cartier left Saint-Malo with two ships and sixty-one men to look for a passage west. Within twenty days they sighted Newfoundland, then sailed along the coast of Labrador, which Cartier called the land God gave to Cain. In the Gulf of the Saint Lawrence they met Mi'kmaq people in canoes, who held up furs on sticks to trade. In September they were home in Saint-Malo.",
  "french:festival": "In medieval French cathedrals the days after Christmas brought the Feast of Fools (Fête des Fous), when young clergy turned the world upside down. At Beauvais on 14 January a girl with a child in her arms was led on a donkey into the church for the Feast of the Ass (Fête de l'Âne), remembering the flight into Egypt. At the end of each part of the Mass, instead of Amen, the congregation answered three times with a hee-haw.",
  "french:games": "Jeu de paume, the game of the palm, was played in medieval France first with the bare hand, later with gloves and rackets, in courtyards and covered courts. In 1316 King Louis X played a hard game at Vincennes, then drank a great deal of cold wine in a cool cellar, fell ill and died. The game remained the passion of kings and townspeople, and by 1600 Paris had hundreds of courts.",
  "french:drought": "Imagined: in a dry summer in Provence the river Rhône runs low and the fields around Tarascon crack open. The town carries its saint, Martha (sainte Marthe), in procession down to the river, with drums and fifes, asking for rain. The farmers walk barefoot behind her. Old women recall the story of how Martha tamed the Tarasque, the monster of the river, and say she can surely bring a little water.",
  "french:quake": "On the evening of 11 June 1909 an earthquake struck Provence, around the villages of Lambesc, Rognes and Saint-Cannat. Houses of old stone collapsed onto families at supper, and some forty-six people died. Soldiers came from Marseille to dig in the rubble. It was the strongest earthquake France had known in a century. Survivors camped in tents in the fields for weeks, listening for aftershocks.",
  "french:winter": "In January 1709 a cold so fierce struck France that it was called the Grand Hiver. The duc de Saint-Simon wrote that wine froze in the barrels and bottles at Versailles, and that the Seine was frozen solid. Walnut and olive trees died, and the winter wheat was killed in the ground. Through the following spring the poor of the countryside went hungry, and many died of famine and cold.",
  "french:plague": "In May 1720 the ship Grand Saint-Antoine brought plague to Marseille. Within months half the city had died. Bishop Belsunce walked through the streets giving the last rites, and the Chevalier Roze took convict labourers to clear the bodies from the esplanade of La Tourette, where they lay in heaps, into old bastions filled with quicklime. A great wall, the mur de la peste, was built across Provence to stop the sickness.",
  "french:wedding": "In French villages a wedding procession walked to church behind the ménétrier, the village fiddler, who played all the way there and back and then for the dancing that went on through the night. The bride's garter was sometimes cut into pieces and shared among the young men. When a widow or widower remarried, the young people might hold a charivari, banging pots and pans under their window until paid off with wine.",

  "jeanne-darc:siege": "From October 1428 the people of Orléans lived behind their walls, ringed by English forts. Townspeople stood watch beside soldiers, women carried stones and boiling water to the ramparts, and the great bell of the belfry warned of every attack. Bread grew dear and then scarce. On 29 April 1429 word spread that the Maid (la Pucelle) had come with a convoy of food; that night the whole town poured into the streets with torches to see her.",
  "jeanne-darc:triumph": "On 8 May 1429 the English lifted the siege of Orléans and marched away. The people and the soldiers went in procession around the walls and to the churches, singing hymns of thanks, with Jeanne among them. The town council decided to repeat the procession every year. It has been held almost every 8 May since, with bells, banners and a girl of Orléans riding as the Maid.",
  "jeanne-darc:build": "In 1481 work began on a great church at Saint-Nicolas-de-Port in Lorraine, to house a finger relic of Saint Nicholas (saint Nicolas) that had drawn pilgrims for centuries, among them, it is said, Jeanne herself before she left for France. Masons raised its two tall towers and its slender pillars, the tallest in Lorraine, over some sixty years. Lorrainers still come each December to honour the saint of children.",
  "jeanne-darc:voyage": "In February 1429 Jeanne left Vaucouleurs with six companions, dressed as a man, to ride to the Dauphin at Chinon. The journey took eleven days through country held by enemies. They rode mostly by night, crossed rivers swollen with winter rain, and slept on the ground. Her companions said later she never complained and wished to hear Mass every day. At last the towers of Chinon rose above the Vienne.",
  "jeanne-darc:festival": "Near Domrémy stood a great beech called the Ladies' Tree (l'Arbre des Dames), by a spring said to cure fevers. Jeanne told her judges that on a Sunday in Lent, called the Sunday of the Fountains (dimanche des Fontaines), the young people of the village went out to the tree, hung it with garlands, ate little loaves baked for the day, drank from the spring, and danced and sang. She said she had made garlands there herself.",
  "jeanne-darc:games": "Imagined: on a summer feast day in a village of Lorraine the young men set up the quilles, the wooden skittles, on the green before the church. Each throws the heavy ball in turn, and the old men argue loudly over every pin. The girls watch from the shade, a fiddler plays, and the winner gets a cake and the right to choose his partner for the first dance of the evening.",
  "jeanne-darc:drought": "Imagined: a dry summer in the Meuse valley, the river shrinking between white stones, the oats short and thin. The priest of Domrémy leads the village out on the Rogation days, walking the boundaries of the fields with cross and banner, chanting the litany of the saints and blessing the crops. Children beat the boundary stones with sticks so they will remember. Everyone looks up at the sky between verses.",
  "jeanne-darc:quake": "On 12 May 1682 an earthquake struck the southern Vosges in Lorraine, around Remiremont and Plombières. Chimneys fell, church vaults cracked, and the great abbey of the canonesses of Remiremont was damaged. People ran out of their houses at night and stayed in the fields, praying to Saint Romaric. It is still the strongest earthquake recorded in that part of the mountains, and villages remembered it for generations.",
  "jeanne-darc:winter": "Imagined: a long winter in Domrémy. Snow lies on the roofs, and the family sits close around the hearth. Jeanne's mother, Isabelle, spins and teaches her daughter the Our Father, the Hail Mary and the Creed, which is all the learning Jeanne ever had. The father mends harness. When the church bell rings for compline across the snow, the girl kneels down, as she always does.",
  "jeanne-darc:plague": "Imagined: plague comes to a village of Lorraine in a hot summer, in the years of war. Houses are shut and marked, and the dead are carried out at night. The villagers vow a chapel to Saint Roch, the pilgrim saint with a dog at his side and a sore on his leg, if he spares the rest. When autumn comes and the sickness passes, they build it with their own hands.",
  "jeanne-darc:wedding": "In 1428 a young man of Toul claimed that Jeanne had promised to marry him, and took her before the church court. Her parents had hoped she would accept. Jeanne, then about sixteen, went to Toul alone and swore that she had made no such promise; she had already vowed her virginity to God. The judge found for her. It was the only wedding ever planned for her, and she refused it.",

  "knights-templar:siege": "From 1189 to 1191 the crusaders besieged Acre, while Saladin's army in turn surrounded their camp. In the winter of 1190 hunger struck the besiegers: a sack of grain cost a fortune, and knights killed their warhorses and ate them, even the entrails. The Templars shared out what they had. Men chewed grass and bones. When ships at last arrived with grain, the camp sang the Te Deum. In July 1191 the city surrendered.",
  "knights-templar:triumph": "On 25 November 1177 a small army of the young king Baldwin IV, sick with leprosy, with some eighty Templars, surprised the much larger army of Saladin at Montgisard. The Templar charge broke the enemy, and Saladin escaped on a racing camel. In Jerusalem the bells rang and the clergy and people sang the Te Deum in the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, and the Templars gave thanks in their own chapel.",
  "knights-templar:build": "In February 1185 Heraclius, Patriarch of Jerusalem, came to London and consecrated the new round church of the Templars beside the Thames, built in the shape of the Church of the Holy Sepulchre. Masons had used Purbeck marble for its slender columns, a style new to England. The Temple Church still stands, with its effigies of knights on the floor, and its choir still sings plainchant.",
  "knights-templar:voyage": "Imagined: spring at Marseille. A Templar ship takes on pilgrims, horses, cloth and silver for the East. For weeks they sail past Sicily and Crete, and every evening the chaplain sings the office of compline on deck while pilgrims kneel among the sacks. One morning a sailor cries out: the low coast of Acre is in sight. The pilgrims weep and sing the Te Deum.",
  "knights-templar:festival": "In Jerusalem the greatest feast was Easter. On Holy Saturday the crowds of pilgrims, Latins, Greeks, Armenians and Syrians, waited in the Church of the Holy Sepulchre (Sanctum Sepulchrum) for the Holy Fire, a flame said to kindle by itself in the tomb. Fulcher of Chartres described their anguish in 1101 when it was late. When it came, it passed from candle to candle, and the church filled with light and singing.",
  "knights-templar:games": "The Templar Rule forbade the brothers to play chess or tables, the dice game later called backgammon, for they led to idleness and betting. It made one small allowance: brothers might play a game of merels (marelles), three or nine men's morris, on boards scratched into stone, if no stakes were laid, and only for amusement. Such boards are still found carved into the benches and walls of their castles.",
  "knights-templar:drought": "In 1185 the rains failed across the land of Jerusalem and famine threatened. The leaders of the kingdom asked Saladin for a truce, and he agreed. Chroniclers record that grain was even brought in to feed the Christian towns. For a season, while the cisterns stood empty and the fields lay dry, the Templars and their neighbours watched the sky for the same rain.",
  "knights-templar:quake": "On 20 May 1202, before dawn, a great earthquake shook the lands along the eastern Mediterranean. In Acre the royal palace and many houses fell, and walls in Tyre crumbled. Templar castles were damaged, and the brothers had to rebuild towers and walls. Pilgrims wrote home that the earth had moved like the sea. The Templars sang the offices in the ruins and set to work.",
  "knights-templar:winter": "In the winter of 1191 to 1192 the crusader army marched up toward Jerusalem through cold rain, hail and snow. Horses died in the mud, food rotted, and armour rusted. At Beit Nuba, within a day's march, the Templars and Hospitallers advised that the city could not be held even if taken. The army turned back. Chroniclers wrote of men weeping and cursing as they went down the hill.",
  "knights-templar:plague": "In 1218 and 1219 the crusaders besieged Damietta in Egypt, with the Templars among them. Oliver of Paderborn wrote of a sickness in the camp: the gums swelled, the teeth loosened, and the legs turned black. Many died in the winter. When the army finally entered the city in November 1219, they found it full of the dead, for disease and famine had struck the defenders too.",
  "knights-templar:wedding": "Templar knights swore chastity, but the Rule allowed married men to join as confratres (confrères), associate brothers. They could not wear the white mantle, which was for celibate knights alone, and they had to promise that at death half their goods would go to the Order. A married man might live with his wife and serve the Temple at the same time, and many families supported it this way.",

  "burgundians:siege": "From July 1474 to June 1475 Charles the Bold besieged the Rhineland city of Neuss for eleven months. The townspeople held the walls through the winter, ate their horses, and shot messages over the river in hollow cannonballs. Inside, a chronicler wrote, the women carried stones to the walls and sang to keep their courage up. The duke finally lifted the siege, and Neuss celebrated its holding out every year afterwards.",
  "burgundians:triumph": "In April 1458 Philip the Good made a joyous entry into Ghent (Gent). The city built stages along his route with tableaux vivants, living pictures staged by actors. One recreated the Van Eyck brothers' Ghent Altarpiece, with live singers and musicians as the angels. Burghers in their best clothes lined the streets, wine flowed, and the duke rode through to cheering, as all the city poured out to see the show.",
  "burgundians:build": "In 1383 Philip the Bold (Philippe le Hardi) founded the Chartreuse de Champmol outside Dijon, a Carthusian monastery to pray for his family. Masons, painters and sculptors worked on it for years. In its cloister Claus Sluter carved the Well of Moses (Puits de Moïse), six prophets around a fountain, so lifelike that they seem about to speak. Pilgrims came to touch the well, and the monks' chant filled the cloister day and night.",
  "burgundians:voyage": "Later legends traced the Burgundians to the island of Bornholm in the Baltic. By 413 they had crossed into the Roman lands and settled on the Rhine, around Worms; after a great defeat in 436, remembered in the Nibelungenlied, the survivors were given new land in Sapaudia, around Lake Geneva, in 443. Families, wagons and cattle moved south again, until the lake and the Alps lay before them.",
  "burgundians:festival": "In Dijon in the fifteenth century, the carnival society called the Mère Folle, Mother Folly, had hundreds of members, from shoemakers to nobles. At carnival they paraded through the streets in green, red and yellow costumes, riding carts, playing drums and fifes, and singing satirical songs in Burgundian dialect about the town's scandals. The Mère Folle herself, a man in a dress, presided over the merriment.",
  "burgundians:games": "In 1449 and 1450 the knight Jacques de Lalaing held the Pas de la Fontaine des Pleurs, the Passage of the Fountain of Tears (Pas de la Fontaine des Pleurs), near Chalon-sur-Saône. A pavilion was set up by a fountain with a painted lady weeping, and any knight could touch one of three shields to challenge him. For a year, on the first day of each month, he fought all comers, and won every time.",
  "burgundians:drought": "Since 1354 the vineyards of Beaune have kept the dates when the grape harvest began. In 1540, a year of terrible drought across Europe, the harvest began in mid-August, earlier than almost any other year. The grapes were small and shrivelled, but the wine was so strong that people remembered it for decades. The vintners watched the parched slopes and gave thanks for every bunch.",
  "burgundians:quake": "In 563, Gregory of Tours and Marius of Avenches wrote, a mountain called Tauredunum collapsed near where the Rhône flows into Lake Geneva (lac Léman). A great wave rushed down the lake, flooding villages along the shore and pouring over the walls of Geneva, killing many people. The bridge and the mills were swept away. The people of the old Burgundian kingdom remembered the year the lake rose up.",
  "burgundians:winter": "On 5 January 1477, in snow and bitter cold, Charles the Bold, Duke of Burgundy, fell in battle outside Nancy. His body was found two days later in a frozen pond, stripped and half eaten by wolves, recognized only by his page and his physician from old scars and his long nails. Burgundians wept for him, and their great duchy, so rich in music, was divided among others.",
  "burgundians:plague": "Imagined: plague comes to Dijon in a hot summer. The rich flee to their country houses; the poor stay. The bells of Saint-Bénigne toll again and again, and the brotherhoods carry the dead to the cemeteries. The townspeople walk in procession behind the relics of the saints, barefoot, singing the litany. When autumn comes and the sickness eases, they light candles of thanks taller than a man.",
  "burgundians:wedding": "On 3 July 1468 Charles the Bold married Margaret of York in Damme, and for nine days Bruges (Brugge) held a feast that astonished Europe. Olivier de la Marche described it: a pie containing musicians, a mechanical whale with sirens singing inside, a giant leading a dromedary, and the Tournament of the Golden Tree (Pas de l'Arbre d'Or) in the market square. The fountains ran with wine, and the citizens of Bruges danced in the streets.",

  "dutch:siege": "In 1574 Leiden was besieged for months, and its people starved; thousands died of hunger and plague. To reach them, the dikes were cut and the land around the city slowly flooded, so that flat-bottomed boats could sail across the fields. On 3 October 1574 the relief fleet reached the city and handed out herring and white bread. Leiden still celebrates 3 October (Leidens Ontzet) with herring, white bread and hutspot, a stew of carrots and onions.",
  "dutch:triumph": "In September 1628 Piet Hein captured the Spanish silver fleet in the bay of Matanzas, off Cuba, without a battle. When his ships came home in January 1629, laden with silver, gold, indigo and cochineal, the whole country celebrated, and the shareholders of the company received a huge dividend. Children still sing the song 'Piet Hein, Piet Hein, zijn naam is klein, zijn daden bennen groot', his name is small, his deeds are great.",
  "dutch:build": "In 1927 work began on the Afsluitdijk, a dam thirty-two kilometres long to close off the Zuiderzee from the North Sea. Men laid mattresses of willow branches on the sea floor and dropped boulder clay from barges. On 28 May 1932 the last gap was closed, and the workers cheered as the two ends met. A monument there reads: Een volk dat leeft, bouwt aan zijn toekomst, a living people builds for its future.",
  "dutch:voyage": "In August 1642 Abel Tasman (Abel Janszoon Tasman) left Batavia with two ships, the Heemskerck and the Zeehaen, to search for a great southern land. On 24 November his men sighted a high wooded coast that he named Anthony van Diemen's Land, now Tasmania. A ship's carpenter swam ashore through the surf to plant a flag on a post. In December they sighted the mountains of a land they named Staten Landt, later New Zealand.",
  "dutch:festival": "On the eve of 5 December Dutch children leave a shoe by the hearth for Sinterklaas, with a carrot or hay for his horse. Jan Steen painted the morning in the 1660s: a little girl clutching her doll and a bucket of sweets, a boy weeping because his shoe holds only a birch rod, the others laughing, and the grandmother beckoning him toward a gift she has hidden behind the bed curtain.",
  "dutch:games": "When the canals and lakes froze in the cold winters of the 1600s, all Holland went out on the ice. Hendrick Avercamp painted the scene again and again: skaters gliding hand in hand, sledges pulled by horses, children falling over, and men playing kolf, hitting a ball across the ice with curved clubs toward a post. Rich and poor shared the same ice, and a koek-en-zopie stall sold hot drinks.",
  "dutch:drought": "Imagined: a long dry summer in the polders. The ditches between the fields shrink to green mud, and the cattle stand at the edges, bellowing. The windmills turn backwards now, pumping water from the river into the land instead of out. In the peat country a fire smoulders underground for weeks, smoking from the earth. The farmers go to church on Sunday and listen hard to the prayer for rain.",
  "dutch:winter": "In 1596 Willem Barentsz and his crew were trapped by ice off Novaya Zemlya in the Arctic. They built a house from driftwood and timbers of their ship, the Behouden Huys, the Saved House, and lived in it through the polar night, burning coal, trapping foxes, and fighting off polar bears. To keep up their spirits they held a Twelfth Night feast. In June 1597 they sailed away in two open boats; Barentsz died on the way.",
  "dutch:plague": "In 1663 and 1664 plague struck Amsterdam, then the busiest port in Europe; some twenty-four thousand people, perhaps one in eight, died. The city set up a plague house (pesthuis) outside the walls, and the sick were carried there in covered stretchers. Ships from infected ports were held offshore. Families shut their doors, and the bells of the Oude Kerk tolled for funerals almost without stopping, while trade, as always, went on.",
  "dutch:wedding": "At a Dutch wedding in the 1600s the guests were given bruidsuikers, sugared almonds, and drank bruidstranen, bride's tears, a spiced brandy with gold leaf floating in it. The bride wore a wreath of flowers or greenery, and the couple's friends decorated the door with garlands. Often a painted wooden chest, the bride's chest (bruidskist), held her linen and was carried in procession to her new house, while musicians played.",

  "spanish:siege": "In 134 to 133 BC the Roman general Scipio Aemilianus surrounded the hill town of Numantia (Numancia), in what is now Castile, with walls and towers. The Celtiberian people inside refused to surrender. As hunger grew, Appian wrote, they ate leather, then grass, then the dead. In the end many chose to die rather than be taken. Cervantes later made their story a tragedy, La Numancia, and Castilians have honoured their courage ever since.",
  "spanish:triumph": "Imagined: the soldiers of a tercio come home to their village in Castile (Castilla) after years in Flanders and Italy. The church bell rings as they appear on the road, thin and brown, their coats patched. Mothers run out to search the faces. In the plaza the mayor opens a skin of wine, a boy plays the dulzaina and the drum, and the old men ask for stories until the moon is high.",
  "spanish:build": "On 23 April 1563 the first stone was laid of El Escorial (San Lorenzo de El Escorial), a monastery, palace and church in the hills north of Madrid. The architects Juan Bautista de Toledo and then Juan de Herrera built it in grey granite, severe and plain. Thousands of stonemasons, carters and labourers worked on it for twenty-one years. On 13 September 1584 the last stone was set, and the monks began singing the daily office there.",
  "spanish:voyage": "On 3 August 1492 three ships, the Santa María, the Pinta and the Niña, sailed from Palos with about ninety men. Weeks passed with no land, and the sailors grew afraid and angry. In the early hours of 12 October a sailor on the Pinta, Rodrigo de Triana, saw a white sandy shore in the moonlight and cried 'Tierra! Tierra!' At dawn they went ashore on an island in the Bahamas.",
  "spanish:festival": "For Corpus Christi in Toledo the streets are hung with tapestries and covered with awnings, and the path is strewn with rosemary and thyme. Giants and dwarfs, the gigantes y cabezudos, dance in the street, and in the past a dragon called the Tarasca, with a doll riding on its back, snapped at the crowd. Then the great silver monstrance of the cathedral passes, and the people fall silent and kneel.",
  "spanish:games": "In the Plaza Mayor of Madrid in the 1600s, festivals brought the juego de cañas, the game of canes, which Castilians had learned from Moorish horsemen. Teams of nobles in bright costumes rode at each other, throwing light cane spears and catching them on leather shields, then wheeling away at full gallop. The balconies were packed with spectators, and the whole city came to watch and to bet.",
  "spanish:drought": "Imagined: no rain since the winter on the plain of La Mancha. The wheat stands short and yellow, and the wells have dropped. The village carries the image of the Virgin (la Virgen) from her hermitage on the hill down to the parish church, in a rogativa for rain, with men bareheaded in the heat and women saying the rosary. They promise she will stay in the village until it rains.",
  "spanish:quake": "On the morning of 1 November 1755, All Saints' Day, the great Lisbon earthquake shook southern Spain too. Church towers cracked in Seville (Sevilla) and Salamanca. An hour later a great sea wave struck the coast at Cádiz and Huelva; in Ayamonte and other towns of the Andalusian coast many people were drowned. At Cádiz, people say, a priest held up a banner of the Virgin at the city gate, and the water stopped.",
  "spanish:winter": "Imagined: December on the high plain of Castile. The wind from the sierra cuts through every coat, and frost whitens the stubble each morning. In the villages it is the time of the matanza, the pig-killing: the whole family gathers, sausages and blood puddings are made, and everyone shares in a long meal. The fire is fed with vine roots, and the old shepherds tell stories of the mountains.",
  "spanish:plague": "In the spring and summer of 1649 plague struck Seville (Sevilla), then one of the richest cities in Europe. Within a few months nearly half its people, perhaps sixty thousand, had died. Carts of bodies rolled through the streets to great pits outside the walls. The painter Murillo lived through it; many of the poor children he later painted were orphans of that year. The city's glory never fully returned.",
  "spanish:wedding": "At a Castilian wedding the groom gives the bride thirteen coins, the arras, often in a small silver box, blessed by the priest. They stand for his promise to care for her and for the home they will share. A cord or lazo may be placed around the couple's shoulders as they kneel. Then the guests throw rice at the church door, and the feast and dancing go on into the night.",

  "portuguese:siege": "From May to September 1384 a Castilian army besieged Lisbon (Lisboa) by land and river. The chronicler Fernão Lopes described the hunger in the city: mothers with nothing to give their children, small boys going through the streets crying for bread, and people eating grass and the hides of animals. Then plague broke out in the besieging camp and spread fast. In September the siege was lifted, and Lisbon thanked God it had held out.",
  "portuguese:triumph": "In the summer of 1499 the ships of Vasco da Gama came home to Lisbon from India, the first to reach it by sea around Africa. Two-thirds of the men had died on the voyage, among them Vasco's brother Paulo. But they brought pepper and cinnamon, and the city held processions of thanks, with bells ringing and the king riding out to greet them. People said the treasures of the East would now come by sea.",
  "portuguese:build": "In 1501, at Belém by the Tagus, work began on the Jerónimos monastery, near the chapel where Vasco da Gama's sailors had prayed the night before they sailed. It was paid for by a tax on the spice trade. The master builders Diogo de Boitaca and João de Castilho covered its stone with carved ropes, anchors, shells and sea creatures. Its monks were charged to pray for the sailors who left from Belém.",
  "portuguese:voyage": "For years Portuguese sailors would not go beyond Cape Bojador (Cabo Bojador) on the African coast; they said the sea boiled there and no one came back. In 1434 Gil Eanes, a squire of Prince Henry, was sent a second time. He sailed far out to sea, then turned back east, and found that he had passed the cape safely. He landed and picked wild rosemary, called Saint Mary's roses (rosas de Santa Maria), to bring back as proof.",
  "portuguese:festival": "On the night of 12 to 13 June Lisbon celebrates (Lisboa) Santo António, its own saint, born in the city around 1195. The old neighbourhoods of Alfama and Mouraria are hung with paper garlands and lights; grills smoke with sardines on bread; and couples dance to accordions in the streets. Young men give their sweethearts a manjerico, a small pot of basil with a paper carnation and a little verse of love.",
  "portuguese:games": "In the hills of Minho young men learned jogo do pau, stick fighting with long staffs of ash or holm oak. At fairs and pilgrimages, rival villages would sometimes test each other with the staff, and good players could hold off several attackers at once. Masters taught it in barns and courtyards. It was a game, a sport and a defence, and an old man of Minho might still show you his favourite move.",
  "portuguese:drought": "Imagined: the Alentejo in August. No rain for months; the wheat fields are bare stubble, the cork oaks stand still in the heat, and the cisterns are low. At evening the men of the village gather in the tavern and sing cante alentejano, slow songs in many voices, one man leading and the others joining, about the land, the heat and the rain that will not come. The sun goes down red.",
  "portuguese:quake": "Before dawn on 22 October 1522 an earthquake shook São Miguel in the Azores (Açores), and a hillside above Vila Franca do Campo, then the island's main town, gave way. A river of earth and mud buried the town as its people slept; perhaps five thousand died. Only a few houses were left. The survivors dug for their families for days, then moved the island's capital to Ponta Delgada and carried their saints with them.",
  "portuguese:winter": "Imagined: January in the Serra da Estrela. Snow covers the high pastures and the shepherds have brought their flocks down to the villages. In the stone houses the women make cheese from the winter ewes' milk, curdled with wild thistle flowers. The shepherds sit by the fire with their dogs, the big mountain dogs of the Estrela, and talk about wolves and the long road up when spring comes.",
  "portuguese:plague": "In 1505, after plague had struck Lisbon, King Manuel asked Venice for a relic of Saint Roch (São Roque), the pilgrim saint who protected against the sickness. When it came, the people built a chapel for it on a hill outside the city, where plague victims were buried; later the church of São Roque grew from it. For centuries Lisbon's people went there in plague years to pray that the sickness would pass them by.",
  "portuguese:wedding": "In the Minho the traditional bride dressed in black: a velvet dress embroidered with black beads, a white lace veil, and around her neck a great weight of gold filigree, hearts, crosses and chains handed down by her mother and grandmother. The gold showed her family's care for her. The procession walked to the chapel with a band of concertinas and drums, and the feast lasted until dawn.",

  "italians:siege": "From October 1529 to August 1530 Florence (Firenze) was besieged by imperial and papal armies. Michelangelo, put in charge of the defences, built earthworks around San Miniato al Monte and had its bell tower wrapped in mattresses of wool so the cannonballs would bounce off. Inside the walls the people ate cats and rats as food ran out, and plague came with the summer. In August the city surrendered, starving but unbowed.",
  "italians:triumph": "Imagined: a Venetian galley rows home into the lagoon of Venice (Venezia) after a long summer at sea. As it passes the Lido, the bells of San Marco ring, and boats crowd out to meet it. The oarsmen, free men of the city, stand up to wave to their families on the Riva degli Schiavoni. That night the taverns of Castello are full of songs, and the women count the pay.",
  "italians:build": "In 1420 Filippo Brunelleschi began the dome of Florence cathedral, Santa Maria del Fiore, without wooden scaffolding from the ground, a feat no one thought possible. He designed ox-driven hoists and laid the bricks in a herringbone pattern. The masons ate their lunch and drank watered wine up on the dome so as not to waste time climbing down. On 25 March 1436 the church was consecrated to Guillaume Du Fay's motet 'Nuper rosarum flores'.",
  "italians:voyage": "Between 1880 and 1915 some four million Italians crossed the Atlantic to America, most of them from the south: peasants from Campania, Calabria (Calàbbria) and Sicily with a trunk, a few words of English and a letter from a cousin. The crossing from Naples (Napoli) took two weeks in steerage. When the Statue of Liberty appeared in New York harbour, passengers wept, cheered and crossed themselves, and mothers held up their children to see.",
  "italians:festival": "Carnival (Carnevale) in Venice (Venezia) once lasted for weeks, from Saint Stephen's Day to Ash Wednesday. Behind masks, the bauta and the moretta, rich and poor, patricians and servants mixed freely in the squares. On Giovedì Grasso, Fat Thursday, a bull was beheaded in the Piazzetta, and acrobats formed human pyramids, the Forze d'Ercole. At midnight on Shrove Tuesday the bells of San Francesco della Vigna rang and the city put its masks away.",
  "italians:games": "On 17 February 1530, during the siege, the young men of Florence played a game of calcio storico in the Piazza Santa Croce, with musicians playing on the church roof so the besieging army could hear. It was a ball game of twenty-seven players a side, a mix of football, rugby and wrestling. Florence still plays it every June in the same square, the four old quarters of the city in their colours.",
  "italians:drought": "Imagined: the dry summer burns the Tavoliere plain of Apulia (Puglia) brown, and the wheat comes up thin. In a village the people take the statue of their patron saint out of the church and carry him around the dusty fields, showing him the cracked earth so he can see for himself. When the rain still doesn't come, some threaten to leave him out in the sun until he listens.",
  "italians:quake": "On 2 February 1703, the feast of Candlemas (la Candelora), a great earthquake struck L'Aquila in the Abruzzo mountains. The church of San Domenico collapsed during Mass, killing hundreds of worshippers who had come to have their candles blessed. Most of the city fell. In the whole region perhaps six thousand people died. The survivors rebuilt their churches, and the city's festivals and processions continued in tents.",
  "italians:winter": "In January 1709 the lagoon of Venice froze so hard that people walked across the ice from the city to the mainland at Mestre. Trees split with a crack like gunfire, the olive groves of Tuscany (Toscana) died, and wolves came down from the Apennines into villages. In the cities the poor burned their furniture to keep warm, and the churches organized soup kitchens to keep the starving alive.",
  "italians:plague": "In 1348 the Black Death came to Florence, and in a few months more than half the city died. Giovanni Boccaccio describes it in the Decameron: bodies piled in the churchyards, families abandoning each other, and seven young women and three young men who left for a villa in the hills, where they told each other a hundred stories to pass ten days while the city died below them.",
  "italians:wedding": "In Renaissance Florence the bride was led to her husband's house on horseback through the streets, and her dowry and trousseau followed in a pair of painted wooden chests, cassoni, decorated with scenes of love and virtue. The guests received confetti, sugared almonds, five for health, wealth, happiness, children and long life. The feast in the husband's house lasted several days, with musicians playing for the dancing.",

  "sicilians:siege": "In 1282, after the Sicilian Vespers (Vespri siciliani), the city of Messina (Missina) was besieged by sea and land. Every citizen helped defend it. Legend tells of two women, Dina and Clarenza, who saw the enemy approaching at night: Dina drove them back with stones, while Clarenza rang the bells of the cathedral to wake the city. They are still remembered in the great astronomical clock of Messina cathedral, ringing the hours.",
  "sicilians:triumph": "In 480 BC the Greek cities of Sicily won a great victory over a Carthaginian army at Himera. Diodorus wrote that the prisoners were divided among the cities and set to work building temples. In Akragas (Agrigento, Girgenti) they raised the vast Temple of Olympian Zeus, its roof held up by stone giants. For years the farmers of Sicily had captive workers in their fields, and the cities held festivals of thanksgiving.",
  "sicilians:build": "From 1174 the Norman king William II built a cathedral at Monreale, above Palermo (Palermu). Greek mosaicists covered its walls with golden images of the whole Bible, and Arab-Sicilian craftsmen made its cloister of paired columns inlaid with glittering stone. Norman, Greek and Arab workers built it together within a few years. When the sun shines through its windows, the whole church glows gold, and the Christ looks down from the apse.",
  "sicilians:voyage": "In 734 BC, Thucydides says, settlers from Corinth under Archias sailed west across the Ionian sea in search of new land. They came first to the coast of Sicily near the smoking mountain of Etna (Mungibeddu), then founded the city of Syracuse (Siracusa, Sarausa) on the island of Ortygia, where a freshwater spring, the fountain of Arethusa (Aretusa), rose by the sea. Their descendants built the greatest Greek city in the west.",
  "sicilians:festival": "Every February Catania celebrates Saint Agatha (Sant'Àita), its patron, martyred there in 251. The city carries her silver reliquary through the streets for three days, pulled on ropes by thousands of men in white robes and black caps, the devoti, shouting 'Cittadini, viva Sant'Àita!' Great carved candelore, giant candle towers, are carried before her by the guilds, swaying and dancing. Fireworks light the sky over Etna.",
  "sicilians:games": "Imagined: on the feast day of a village in the hills of Sicily, the young men race donkeys down the main street, riding bareback while the crowd cheers and the band plays. Then comes the greasy pole, with a ham and a cheese tied at the top. The boys climb, slip, climb on each other's shoulders, and finally one reaches the prize and holds it up to the cheers of the village.",
  "sicilians:drought": "Imagined: no rain in a Sicilian village since spring. The wheat on the hills is burnt, and the cisterns are empty. The people carry the statue of their patron saint in procession, and when still no rain falls, they turn the saint to face the sea, or take away his silver crown, scolding him like a family member who has forgotten his duty. When it finally rains, they bring him back with music.",
  "sicilians:quake": "In the night of 14 to 15 January 1968 an earthquake destroyed the towns of the Belice valley in western Sicily (Sicilia): Gibellina, Poggioreale, Salaparuta, Montevago. Some four hundred people died, and tens of thousands slept in tents in the winter rain. Many waited years in huts for new homes. On the ruins of old Gibellina the artist Alberto Burri later poured a great field of white concrete, the Cretto, following the lines of the lost streets.",
  "sicilians:winter": "Through the winter, men of the villages around Etna (Mungibeddu) climbed the mountain to gather snow and pack it into stone pits called neviere, covering it with ash and straw. In summer they dug it out and carried it down on mules to the cities, where it cooled the drinks of Palermo and Catania and made granita. The cold of the mountain winter became the sweetness of the Sicilian summer.",
  "sicilians:plague": "In the spring of 1743 a ship from Greece brought plague to Messina (Missina). It spread through the city faster than anyone could count. In a few months some forty thousand people died, perhaps two in every three. The rich fled to the hills, priests buried the dead in trenches by the sea, and the survivors carried the Madonna della Lettera, the city's protector, through the empty streets, praying for the sickness to end.",
  "sicilians:wedding": "In old Sicily the bride's trousseau, the corredo, was laid out in her family's house for all the neighbours to see before the wedding: sheets, towels and nightgowns, all embroidered by her and her mother over years. On the wedding day the bride, the zita, walked to church with her family. At the feast there was music and dancing, and the guests ate cannoli, cassata and almond sweets late into the night.",

  "maltese:siege": "By August 1942 Malta was starving under siege; the bread ration was cut and the Victory Kitchens served thin soup. A convoy was fought through, and on 15 August, the feast of Santa Marija, the crippled tanker Ohio was towed into Grand Harbour (il-Port il-Kbir) lashed between two destroyers, its deck almost awash. Crowds lined the bastions of Valletta (il-Belt Valletta), cheering, praying and weeping. Maltese still call it the Santa Marija Convoy (il-Konvoj ta' Santa Marija).",
  "maltese:triumph": "On 8 September 1565, after the Great Siege (l-Assedju l-Kbir), the Ottoman fleet sailed away. The bells of every church still standing rang, and Grand Master Jean de Valette and the people of Birgu (il-Birgu) went to the church of San Lawrenz to sing the Te Deum. Starved and wounded, the people came out from the ruins of the town. On that day, Il-Vittorja, Maltese still hold boat races in Grand Harbour.",
  "maltese:build": "The Ġgantija temples of Gozo (Għawdex), built over five thousand years ago, are among the oldest stone buildings in the world. Their great blocks weigh many tons. Gozitans told the story that a giantess named Sansuna built them, carrying the stones on her head while nursing a baby, and that she fed on beans and water. Archaeologists say whole communities hauled the stones together, rolling them on round stones.",
  "maltese:voyage": "Imagined: in the 1950s a family from Żejtun boards a ship in Grand Harbour (il-Port il-Kbir), bound for Australia. Relatives wave from the bastions, and the father's mother weeps and gives him a small picture of the Madonna. After weeks at sea, across the Indian Ocean, the coast of Australia rises out of the morning haze. The children rush to the rail, and the mother says the rosary in Maltese.",
  "maltese:festival": "In summer every Maltese village holds its festa for its patron saint. The streets are hung with banners and lights, the church glows with candles and red damask, and brass bands march through the streets. On the main day the statue of the saint is carried out through crowds and petals, and fireworks, made by the village's own volunteers, crack overhead. Everyone eats nougat, qubbajt, from the street stalls.",
  "maltese:games": "On the feast of Saint Julian (San Ġiljan) and other summer festas, young men try the ġostra: a greasy pole jutting out over the harbour. They run along it to grab a flag at the end, slipping and falling into the sea while the crowd cheers. On 8 September the regatta (ir-Regatta) brings rowing races in Grand Harbour between towns, the boats painted in their colours, with crowds on the bastions.",
  "maltese:drought": "Malta has no rivers, and in dry summers Valletta (il-Belt Valletta) depended on cisterns and water carriers. In 1610 Grand Master Alof de Wignacourt began an aqueduct to bring spring water from the hills near Mdina (l-Imdina) to the new city. On 21 April 1615 the water reached Valletta and flowed from the fountain in the main square. Crowds came to drink and give thanks.",
  "maltese:quake": "On 11 January 1693 a great earthquake struck south-eastern Sicily and was felt across Malta. In Mdina (l-Imdina) the old cathedral of Saint Paul was badly damaged, and many houses fell. No one in Malta died, but the people were terrified. The architect Lorenzo Gafà rebuilt the cathedral in the Baroque style, with the great dome that still rises above the walls of the old city.",
  "maltese:winter": "Imagined: January, and the gregale, the north-east wind, drives grey waves into the harbours. The fishermen of Marsaxlokk pull their luzzu boats up onto the shore and mend their nets, the painted eyes on the bows watching the storm. In the evening the men sit in the band club and an old man sings għana, the improvised folk song, while another answers him on the guitar.",
  "maltese:plague": "In 1813 plague came to Malta on a ship from Alexandria. It spread through Valletta (il-Belt Valletta) and the villages; some four thousand five hundred people died. Houses were shut and sealed, the sick were taken to the lazaretto on Manoel Island, and those who died were buried in lime pits. People prayed to Saint Roch (San Rokku). When the sickness passed, they built chapels of thanks.",
  "maltese:wedding": "Imagined: an old-style Maltese wedding in a village. The bride's mother has prepared her linen and furniture for years. The wedding is held in the bride's parish church, the ringing bells announcing it to the village, and the bride walks there with her father. Afterwards the families gather at home for a feast, with pastizzi, rabbit stew and sweets, and the men sing għana late into the night.",
  "britons:showcase": "Greek and Roman writers describe Celtic bards who praised and mocked their lords while playing the lyre. In medieval Wales (Cymru) the bard and the harper held honoured places at court, and in 1176 the Lord Rhys gathered poets and musicians at Cardigan (Aberteifi) for a contest remembered as the first eisteddfod. Gerald of Wales, writing about 1190, praised Welsh part-singing and named the harp, the crwth and the pipe. The Robert ap Huw manuscript, copied around 1613, keeps old harp music in its own tablature. The triple harp arrived in the 17th century and became the Welsh harp; the crwth and the pibgorn hornpipe faded by about 1800 and are played again today. Cerdd dant, verse sung against a harp air, still fills the eisteddfod stage.",
  "english:showcase": "The earliest English music is heard through finds and pages: a lyre buried at Sutton Hoo in the early 600s, and the round 'Sumer is icumen in', copied in the 13th century and one of the oldest canons known. Minstrels played fiddle, gittern, psaltery and bagpipe; Chaucer's Miller leads the pilgrims out of town on his pipes. Around 1400 the Chapel Royal's repertory was gathered in the Old Hall manuscript, and the Agincourt carol, 'Deo gratias Anglia', was sung soon after. John Dunstable's sweet thirds and sixths, the 'English manner', were admired across the Channel. Clarions and nakers sounded for processions and tournaments, church bells marked the hours, and the sackbut joined the town bands. Today carols, change-ringing and revived consorts carry that sound on.",
  "house-of-lancaster:showcase": "Around 1500 the Eton Choirbook gathered soaring English polyphony for many voices, and Henry VIII's court kept dozens of musicians; an inventory made at his death lists scores of recorders, viols, lutes and shawms. Thomas Tallis and William Byrd wrote for church and chamber, and viol consorts played fantasias and In Nomines in great houses. Towns paid bands of waits, with shawms and sackbuts, to play in the streets and at civic feasts. 'Greensleeves' was registered as a ballad in 1580, and John Dowland's First Booke of Songes (1597) made the lute song famous. The sound faded in the 18th century, but from the late 1800s Arnold Dolmetsch rebuilt lutes, viols and recorders, and cathedral choirs and early-music consorts play this repertory today.",
  "celts:showcase": "Iron Age Celts blew the carnyx, a tall bronze war-trumpet with an animal head; pieces were found at Deskford in Scotland and Tintignac in Gaul. Late Bronze Age Ireland left many cast horns, and Old Irish texts name the dord. The early lyre gave way to the wire-strung harp (cláirseach, clàrsach), played with the fingernails by hereditary harpers; the Trinity College and Queen Mary harps survive from the late Middle Ages. Turlough O'Carolan (Toirdhealbhach Ó Cearbhalláin) composed for patrons into the 1730s, and Edward Bunting wrote down the last old harpers' tunes at Belfast in 1792. The Highland pipes (pìob mhòr) and their piobaireachd, the fiddle, the uilleann pipes and the bodhrán came later. Today sessions, sean-nós singing and Hebridean waulking songs (òrain luaidh) keep the music alive.",
  "franks:showcase": "Little is known of early Frankish music beyond lyres like those found in early medieval graves north of the Alps. In the 750s Pippin and then Charlemagne had Roman chant taught to their singers, and it blended with the older Gallican chant into what we call Gregorian chant. In 757 a Byzantine emperor sent Pippin an organ, a marvel in the West. In the 800s scribes began to write melodies with neumes, and the treatise Musica enchiriadis described organum, the first written Western polyphony. Later came the olifant, the ivory horn that Roland blows in the Chanson de Roland, the vièle, and the organistrum, carved at Santiago in 1188. Monks at Solesmes revived the chant in the 19th century, and it is sung today.",
  "french:showcase": "Medieval French music grew from two sources: the troubadours and trouvères, who sang love songs to the vièle, and the cathedral of Notre-Dame, where Léonin and Pérotin built great organum around 1200. Guillaume de Machaut wrote songs and a complete Mass in the 14th century. At the court, the Vingt-quatre Violons du Roi became the king's string band in the 1620s, and Jean-Baptiste Lully made them famous; Couperin and Rameau wrote for the clavecin (harpsichord). Courtiers played the musette and the vielle à roue (hurdy-gurdy) in pastoral dress, while hunting horns (cors de chasse) called through the forests and fifes and drums marched with the guards. Today the hurdy-gurdy and cabrette play bourrées in the Auvergne and Berry, and hunting-horn players still meet.",
  "jeanne-darc:showcase": "In the 8th century Bishop Chrodegang brought Roman chant to Metz, and its singing school became one of the most admired in the Frankish lands; the 'Lorraine' or Messine neumes, a way of writing chant, take their name from it. Through the Middle Ages the region's abbeys and cathedrals sang the Office and the Mass, with organs and church bells marking the hours. By the 15th century, Jeanne d'Arc's time, minstrels played vièle, psaltery, gittern and recorder, and buisines and clarions sounded for armies and entries into towns. Jeanne herself said she heard her voices most often when the bells rang. No music from her village survives, so the piece is a reconstruction; plainchant is still sung in the region's churches.",
  "knights-templar:showcase": "Gregorian chant (cantus planus) grew from the psalm-singing of early Christian communities and was shaped in the 8th and 9th centuries into one repertory sung across Latin Europe. Monks first wrote it in neumes; around 1025 Guido of Arezzo taught singers to read from lines on a staff and named the notes ut, re, mi. Organum added a second voice, from the Winchester Troper to Léonin and Pérotin at Notre-Dame. The military orders of the 12th century, the Templars among them, sang the daily Office like monks. The rebec came from the Arabic rabāb (رباب) and the nakers from the naqqāra (نقارة). After centuries of revisions, monks at Solesmes restored the old melodies in the 19th century, and abbeys still sing them daily.",
  "burgundians:showcase": "The Valois dukes of Burgundy, from the 1360s to 1477, kept one of the richest chapels in Europe. Gilles Binchois served Philip the Good, Guillaume Dufay wrote for his court, and Antoine Busnoys for Charles the Bold. Their chansons and Masses opened the age of Franco-Flemish polyphony. Court music was divided into haute, the loud alta capella of shawms, bombardes and sackbuts for dancing and outdoors, and basse, the soft harp, lute, recorder, vielle and portative organ for the chamber. At the Feast of the Pheasant in 1454, chroniclers wrote, musicians played from inside a giant pie. Later composers trained in this tradition worked across Europe; the shawm still sounds in the Breton bombarde and the Catalan cobla.",
  "dutch:showcase": "The Low Countries trained many of the great polyphonists of the 15th and 16th centuries, such as Jacob Obrecht, and cast bells for every church tower. In the 1640s the Hemony brothers, working with the blind carillonneur Jacob van Eyck of Utrecht, learned to tune bells precisely, and the tuned carillon (beiaard) was born. Van Eyck also played recorder variations for strollers in a churchyard and printed them as Der Fluyten Lust-hof. Jan Pieterszoon Sweelinck, organist of the Oude Kerk in Amsterdam, played public recitals and taught organists from far away. Psalms were sung in church, the cittern and lute played at home, and Antwerp's Ruckers family built harpsichords. Today carillons ring across Dutch towns and street organs (draaiorgels) play in the squares.",
  "spanish:showcase": "The Cantigas de Santa María, made at the court of Alfonso X in the 13th century, show dozens of musicians in their miniatures, playing lutes, rebecs, bagpipes, harps and drums, many of them from al-Andalus: the laúd comes from al-ʿūd (العود), the atabal from aṭ-ṭabl (الطبل). In the 16th century vihuela players such as Luis de Milán printed books of fantasias, while cathedral ministriles played chirimías and sacabuches beside choirs singing Morales, Guerrero and Victoria. The five-course Spanish guitar spread in the 17th century, taught in Gaspar Sanz's book of 1674. In 19th-century Andalusia, Gitano singers and guitarists shaped flamenco. Today the guitar, castanets, the Galician gaita and the Basque txistu with its drum are all living traditions.",
  "portuguese:showcase": "The oldest Portuguese songs are the cantigas de amigo of the 13th century, sung in Galician-Portuguese; seven by Martin Codax survive on a single parchment, six with their music. King Dinis was himself a trovador. In the 16th and 17th centuries the cathedral and monastery choirs of Évora and Lisbon sang the polyphony of Duarte Lobo and Manuel Cardoso, while trumpets and drums sailed with the caravels. Sailors and settlers carried the small cavaquinho to Madeira and beyond, where it became an ancestor of the ukulele. In Lisbon's port quarters in the 19th century, fado appeared, sung to the viola and the twelve-string guitarra portuguesa. Today fado, the men's cante alentejano and the bombos of the northern festas are all alive.",
  "italians:showcase": "Around 1025 Guido of Arezzo taught singers to read notes on a staff, and in 14th-century Florence the blind organist Francesco Landini wrote ballate and madrigals. In the 16th century Andrea and Giovanni Gabrieli set choirs, cornetti and trombones against each other across San Marco in Venice. Opera was born in Florence around 1600, and Monteverdi's Orfeo was staged at Mantua in 1607, with theorbos, viols and a continuo. In Cremona the Amati, Stradivari and Guarneri families made the violins still prized today, and Corelli and Vivaldi turned the violin into the voice of the concerto. Naples gave the mandolin its classic form in the 18th century, and the 19th belonged to opera. Today Cremona's workshops and Italy's opera houses carry that music on.",
  "sicilians:showcase": "Greek settlers in Sicily played the aulos (αὐλός) and the lyre, and later the island's Arab rulers called it Ṣiqilliya (صقلية) and brought their lutes and drums to its towns. Under the Norman kings of the 12th century, Arab, Greek and Latin musicians served the court at Palermo, though none of their music was written down. What lived on was carried by shepherds and townsfolk: the zampogna bagpipe and ciaramella shawm, played at Christmas novenas; the friscalettu cane flute; and the marranzanu jaw harp. Confraternities sang lamentations in Holy Week, the cantastorie sang news and legends in the squares, and the tarantella set feet moving. Today mandolins, tamburelli, festa drums and church bells fill the island's feasts.",
  "maltese:showcase": "Almost nothing is known of the music of Malta's prehistoric temple builders. Centuries of Arab rule left the Maltese language, and with it the old folk instruments: the żaqq goatskin bagpipe, the tanbur frame drum, the żafżafa friction drum and the flejguta cane flute. The Knights of Saint John, who held the islands from 1530 to 1798, brought Baroque church music, and the cathedral at Mdina (l-Imdina) keeps a large archive of scores from that time. In the 19th century villages founded band clubs, and the festa brass band became the sound of summer feasts. Għana, sung verse over guitars, includes spirtu pront, an improvised song duel. Today the bands, parish choirs and organs play on, and the żaqq has been revived.",
});
